(window.CONTENT_PARTS = window.CONTENT_PARTS || {})["notes-final"] = {"acute-pain-complex":"<h2 id=\"special-groups\">Special groups</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Considerations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Children and infants</td><td>Age-appropriate tools (FLACC, faces), weight-based dosing, regional techniques, nurse-controlled analgesia</td></tr><tr><td>Older people</td><td>Cognitive impairment (PAINAD, Abbey), reduced clearance, delirium risk, NSAID caution</td></tr><tr><td>Communication difficulties, learning disability</td><td>Carers' knowledge, behaviour-based tools</td></tr><tr><td>Unconscious / critically ill</td><td>Behavioural Pain Scale, CPOT; analgesia-first sedation</td></tr><tr><td>Renal or hepatic impairment</td><td>Drug choice and dose adjustment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long-term opioid users</strong></td><td>Continue baseline opioid; expect higher needs; ketamine; regional; avoid withdrawal; plan discharge tapering</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Buprenorphine</strong></td><td>Usually continue (especially low doses), add full agonists and regional; specialist advice for high doses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Methadone</strong></td><td>Continue; consider QT; add multimodal analgesia</td></tr><tr><td>Substance misuse</td><td>Non-judgemental; treat pain adequately; manage withdrawal; addiction services</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"complex-analgesic-needs\">Complex analgesic needs</h2>\n<p>Opioid-induced hyperalgesia (increasing pain despite increasing opioids), neuropathic components after surgery (gabapentinoids short-term in selected patients), IV <strong>ketamine</strong> infusions (low dose), <strong>IV lidocaine</strong> infusions (abdominal surgery; with monitoring and dose limits), regional catheters, and acute pain team review.</p>\n<h2 id=\"persistent-chronic-post-surgical-pain\">Persistent (chronic) post-surgical pain</h2>\n<ul><li>Pain persisting at least 3 months after surgery, not present before or different in character. Common after <strong>amputation, thoracotomy, mastectomy, inguinal hernia repair, cardiac surgery and knee arthroplasty</strong> (10–50% for some procedures).</li><li><strong>Risk factors:</strong> preoperative pain, psychological factors (anxiety, catastrophising), younger age, female sex, surgical nerve injury, <strong>severe acute postoperative pain</strong>, radiotherapy and chemotherapy.</li><li><strong>Reducing risk:</strong> nerve-sparing surgical techniques, good perioperative analgesia (regional where appropriate), identifying at-risk patients, early review of persistent pain, and transitional pain services.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CPSP risk factors and common operations; buprenorphine management; opioid-induced hyperalgesia.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient on 16 mg sublingual buprenorphine for a laparotomy: plan analgesia with the acute pain team.</li></ul></div>","aki-rrt-icu":"<h2 id=\"acute-kidney-injury\">Acute kidney injury</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>KDIGO staging</strong> (creatinine rise and urine output). Causes: prerenal (hypoperfusion), intrinsic (ATN from sepsis and ischaemia, nephrotoxins, glomerulonephritis, rhabdomyolysis, contrast), post-renal (obstruction — exclude with ultrasound and catheter).</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> optimise haemodynamics and volume (avoid overload), stop nephrotoxins, adjust drug doses, treat hyperkalaemia and acidosis.</li><li><strong>Indications for renal replacement therapy (RRT):</strong> refractory hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload, uraemic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy), and certain poisonings (lithium, methanol, salicylates).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"renal-replacement-therapy\">Renal replacement therapy</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Modes:</strong> continuous (CVVH — convection; CVVHD — diffusion; CVVHDF — both) in unstable ICU patients; <strong>intermittent haemodialysis</strong> (faster, more haemodynamic stress); peritoneal dialysis (less common in adult ICU).</li><li><strong>Convection</strong> (solvent drag) removes middle molecules better; <strong>diffusion</strong> removes small molecules (urea, potassium).</li><li><strong>Anticoagulation of the circuit:</strong> <strong>regional citrate</strong> (binds calcium in the circuit; monitor ionised calcium; citrate accumulation in liver failure) or heparin.</li><li><strong>Drug dosing</strong> depends on the mode and effluent rate: drugs with small Vd, low protein binding and renal elimination are removed (e.g. many antibiotics — dose to maintain levels; therapeutic drug monitoring).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"liver-failure\">Liver failure</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Acute liver failure</strong> (e.g. paracetamol overdose, viral, drug-induced): encephalopathy, coagulopathy, hypoglycaemia, cerebral oedema (raised ICP), infection, renal failure, hyperdynamic circulation. Early referral to a liver unit (King's College criteria for transplant).</li><li><strong>Acute-on-chronic liver failure:</strong> decompensated cirrhosis with variceal bleeding (terlipressin, antibiotics, endoscopy, TIPSS), SBP, hepatorenal syndrome (albumin and terlipressin).</li><li><strong>Porphyria:</strong> acute attacks precipitated by drugs (barbiturates, some anticonvulsants), fasting and infection; use safe-drug lists; haem arginate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"gastrointestinal-problems\">Gastrointestinal problems</h2>\n<p>Nausea and vomiting, ileus and gastric stasis (prokinetics), <strong>pancreatitis</strong> (severity scoring, fluids, analgesia, early enteral feeding, managing complications), bowel obstruction, swallowing disorders after prolonged intubation, stress ulcer prophylaxis, and the gut mucosal barrier (bacterial translocation).</p>\n<h2 id=\"nutrition-in-critical-illness\">Nutrition in critical illness</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Enteral feeding</strong> preferred, started within 24–48 hours when possible; <strong>post-pyloric</strong> feeding for gastric intolerance.</li><li><strong>Parenteral nutrition</strong> when enteral feeding is not possible or inadequate (timing debated).</li><li><strong>Energy</strong> about 20–25 kcal/kg/day (lower early), <strong>protein</strong> 1.2–2 g/kg/day; avoid <strong>overfeeding</strong> (hyperglycaemia, raised CO<sub>2</sub> production, hepatic steatosis, azotaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia).</li><li><strong>Refeeding syndrome</strong> (phosphate, potassium, magnesium, thiamine).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> RRT modes; citrate anticoagulation; drug removal; nutrition targets.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> discussing starting RRT for a septic patient with the renal team and the family.</li></ul></div>","anticoag-anaemia-pom":"<h2 id=\"patient-blood-management-pbm\">Patient blood management (PBM)</h2>\n<p>Three pillars: <strong>(1) optimise the patient's own red cell mass</strong> (detect and treat anaemia before surgery), <strong>(2) minimise blood loss</strong> (stop antithrombotics appropriately, surgical technique, tranexamic acid, cell salvage, point-of-care testing to guide products), <strong>(3) optimise tolerance of anaemia</strong> (restrictive transfusion thresholds, oxygen delivery).</p>\n<h2 id=\"preoperative-anaemia\">Preoperative anaemia</h2>\n<ul><li>Common (a third of major surgery patients) and associated with worse outcomes and more transfusion.</li><li><strong>Investigate:</strong> full blood count, ferritin, transferrin saturation, CRP, B<sub>12</sub> and folate, renal function; consider GI investigation for iron deficiency.</li><li><strong>Treat:</strong> oral iron if time allows (weeks), <strong>IV iron</strong> if surgery is within about 6 weeks or oral iron fails; treat B<sub>12</sub>/folate deficiency; erythropoiesis-stimulating agents in selected cases.</li><li><strong>Postoperative anaemia:</strong> IV iron and restrictive transfusion (international consensus 2018).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"anticoagulants-and-antiplatelets\">Anticoagulants and antiplatelets</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Balance thrombosis risk</strong> (e.g. mechanical mitral valve, recent VTE, recent stroke, AF with high CHA<sub>2</sub>DS<sub>2</sub>-VASc) against <strong>bleeding risk</strong> (procedure type, neuraxial block).</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> stop about 5 days before; bridge with LMWH only for high thrombotic risk; check INR (≤ 1.5 for most surgery; lower for neuraxial); reverse urgently with PCC and vitamin K.</li><li><strong>DOACs:</strong> stop 1–3 days (or more for dabigatran with poor renal function and high-bleeding-risk or neuraxial procedures) before; no bridging; restart when haemostasis allows.</li><li><strong>Antiplatelets:</strong> aspirin usually continued; clopidogrel/prasugrel/ticagrelor stopped (5–7 days) if safe — never stop DAPT for recent stents without cardiology advice.</li><li><strong>Neuraxial blocks:</strong> follow the Association of Anaesthetists/OAA/RA-UK guidance on timing (see the Primary note on anticoagulants).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"refusal-of-blood\">Refusal of blood</h2>\n<p>Jehovah's Witnesses: discuss in detail which products and techniques are acceptable (cell salvage, albumin, clotting factor concentrates vary by individual), document an advance decision, optimise haemoglobin, plan meticulous haemostasis and early escalation. A competent adult's refusal is legally binding even if death may result; children's treatment may require legal involvement.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> PBM pillars; iron studies; DOAC stopping times; INR thresholds.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> discussing blood products with a Jehovah's Witness before a hip replacement.</li></ul></div>","aortic-surgery":"<h2 id=\"the-vascular-patient\">The vascular patient</h2>\n<p>High prevalence of <strong>ischaemic heart disease</strong>, hypertension, COPD (smoking), diabetes, CKD and frailty. Assessment includes functional capacity (CPET is common before open repair), echocardiography, renal function, and a multidisciplinary decision between open repair, EVAR or surveillance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aortic-cross-clamping\">Aortic cross-clamping</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Clamp on (infrarenal)</th><th>Clamp off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Afterload</td><td><strong>↑↑</strong> — hypertension, LV strain, ischaemia</td><td><strong>↓↓</strong> — hypotension</td></tr><tr><td>Preload</td><td>↑ (blood redistributed above the clamp)</td><td>↓</td></tr><tr><td>Distal tissues</td><td>Ischaemia, anaerobic metabolism</td><td><strong>Washout</strong> of acid, potassium, lactate, and inflammatory mediators → myocardial depression, vasodilation</td></tr><tr><td>Management</td><td>Deepen anaesthesia, vasodilators (GTN), avoid hypertension and ischaemia</td><td>Volume loading before release, <strong>gradual release</strong> (communicate with the surgeon), vasopressors, correct acidosis and calcium</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Suprarenal and supracoeliac clamps cause more marked changes and greater renal and visceral ischaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"organ-protection\">Organ protection</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renal:</strong> maintain perfusion and volume, minimise clamp time, avoid nephrotoxins; no drug has proven renal protective benefit (mannitol and dopamine are not recommended as protectors).</li><li><strong>Spinal cord</strong> (thoracoabdominal and complex endovascular repairs): the <strong>artery of Adamkiewicz</strong> may be compromised → paraplegia. Protection: <strong>CSF drainage</strong> (lumbar drain, keeping CSF pressure about ≤ 10–15 mmHg), maintaining MAP (spinal cord perfusion pressure = MAP − CSF pressure), distal perfusion, hypothermia, and neurological monitoring (MEPs).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"open-repair\">Open repair</h2>\n<p>Arterial and central lines, cardiac output monitoring, <strong>thoracic epidural</strong> (in selected patients) or other analgesia, cell salvage, warming, tranexamic acid, and critical care afterwards. Complications: bleeding, MI, AKI, bowel ischaemia, spinal cord ischaemia, respiratory failure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endovascular-repair-evar\">Endovascular repair (EVAR)</h2>\n<p>Less physiological stress and lower early mortality, but lifelong surveillance and endoleak risk. Anaesthesia: local anaesthesia with sedation, regional, or GA. Issues: contrast nephropathy, radiation, sudden conversion to open repair, and (complex/branched grafts) spinal cord ischaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ruptured-aaa\">Ruptured AAA</h2>\n<ul><li>A surgical emergency: <strong>permissive hypotension</strong> (e.g. systolic about 70–90 mmHg, conscious patient) until the aorta is controlled; minimal crystalloid; blood products via major haemorrhage protocol; tranexamic acid; large-bore access.</li><li><strong>Prepare and drape before induction</strong> (induction can cause cardiovascular collapse as sympathetic tone and tamponade are lost); the surgeon ready to clamp. <strong>EVAR under local anaesthesia</strong> where anatomy allows (lower mortality in some studies).</li><li>Afterwards: abdominal compartment syndrome, AKI, coagulopathy, multi-organ failure.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> cross-clamp physiology; spinal cord perfusion pressure; permissive hypotension targets.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient with a ruptured AAA arrives in theatre: brief the team and explain your induction plan.</li></ul></div>","ards-ventilation":"<h2 id=\"respiratory-failure\">Respiratory failure</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Type 1</strong> (hypoxaemic; PaO<sub>2</sub> &lt; 8 kPa, normal or low PaCO<sub>2</sub>): V/Q mismatch, shunt (pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, PE).</li><li><strong>Type 2</strong> (hypercapnic; PaCO<sub>2</sub> &gt; 6.5 kPa): pump failure (neuromuscular disease, drugs, chest wall disease, fatigue, COPD).</li><li><strong>Support:</strong> oxygen, <strong>HFNO</strong>, <strong>CPAP</strong> (hypoxaemia, pulmonary oedema), <strong>NIV</strong> (BiPAP: hypercapnic COPD exacerbations, obesity hypoventilation, neuromuscular disease), invasive ventilation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ards-berlin-definition\">ARDS (Berlin definition)</h2>\n<p>Acute onset (within a week), bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusions or collapse, not fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload, and hypoxaemia on at least 5 cmH<sub>2</sub>O PEEP: <strong>P/F ratio ≤ 40 kPa mild, ≤ 26.7 moderate, ≤ 13.3 severe</strong> (300/200/100 mmHg). Causes: pneumonia, sepsis, aspiration, trauma, pancreatitis, transfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lung-protective-ventilation\">Lung-protective ventilation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight</strong> (4–8), <strong>plateau pressure ≤ 30 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong>, driving pressure (plateau − PEEP) ideally &lt; 15 cmH<sub>2</sub>O, <strong>PEEP</strong> titrated (tables or individualised), permissive hypercapnia (pH &gt; 7.20–7.25), oxygen targets (SpO<sub>2</sub> 88–95%).</li><li><strong>Prone positioning</strong> for at least 16 hours a day in moderate–severe ARDS (P/F &lt; 20 kPa) reduces mortality (PROSEVA).</li><li><strong>Neuromuscular blockade</strong> in early severe ARDS (selective use), conservative fluid strategy once shock resolves, avoid high-frequency oscillation.</li><li><strong>Rescue:</strong> <strong>VV-ECMO</strong> in specialist centres for refractory hypoxaemia or hypercapnia; inhaled nitric oxide or prostacyclin as temporary rescue.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"obstructive-airway-disease\">Obstructive airway disease</h2>\n<p>Severe <strong>asthma</strong> and <strong>COPD</strong> on the ventilator: low rates, long expiratory times, small tidal volumes, low PEEP (or matched to intrinsic PEEP in COPD), watch for <strong>dynamic hyperinflation</strong> (hypotension, rising plateau pressure — disconnect to decompress), bronchodilators, steroids; volatile anaesthetics and ketamine for refractory asthma; heliox; ECMO.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-gases-and-environments\">Other gases and environments</h2>\n<p>Nitric oxide (pulmonary vasodilator; methaemoglobin and NO<sub>2</sub> monitoring), heliox (upper airway obstruction), the effects of altitude and hyperbaric oxygen (see Primary).</p>\n<h2 id=\"postoperative-ventilation\">Postoperative ventilation</h2>\n<p>Indications: inadequate airway protection, inadequate gas exchange or respiratory effort, cardiovascular instability, hypothermia, major surgery with fluid shifts, planned (e.g. free flap airway oedema, major trauma), neurological reasons (raised ICP). Weigh the benefits of a controlled wake-up against ventilator-associated complications.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Berlin criteria; protective ventilation targets; prone positioning evidence.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a ventilated patient with pneumonia has a P/F ratio of 12 kPa on FiO<sub>2</sub> 0.9: talk through your next steps.</li></ul></div>","burns-environmental":"<h2 id=\"burns\">Burns</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> total body surface area (TBSA) by the <strong>Lund and Browder chart</strong> (accounts for age) or rule of nines (adults); depth (superficial, partial-thickness, full-thickness). Referral criteria to burns services.</li><li><strong>Airway burns and inhalation injury:</strong> suspect with burns in an enclosed space, facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot in the mouth, hoarseness, stridor. <strong>Early intubation</strong> (uncut tube, large size) before swelling makes it impossible.</li><li><strong>Carbon monoxide:</strong> high-flow oxygen (COHb half-life about 4–5 h in air, about 1 h on 100% O<sub>2</sub>); SpO<sub>2</sub> is falsely normal; co-oximetry. <strong>Cyanide</strong> (burning plastics): lactic acidosis — hydroxocobalamin.</li><li><strong>Fluid resuscitation</strong> (for burns &gt; about 15% TBSA in adults, 10% in children): the <strong>Parkland formula</strong>: 4 mL/kg/%TBSA of crystalloid over 24 h, half in the first 8 h <strong>from the time of the burn</strong>. Titrate to urine output (0.5 mL/kg/h adults, 1 mL/kg/h children) to avoid over-resuscitation (\"fluid creep\": compartment syndromes, pulmonary oedema).</li><li><strong>Other:</strong> escharotomy for circumferential burns, temperature control, analgesia, early enteral feeding, infection.</li><li><strong>Anaesthesia for burns surgery:</strong> difficult airway and access, massive blood loss during excision (TXA, adrenaline infiltration), heat loss, <strong>suxamethonium contraindicated from about 24–48 h</strong> to over a year (hyperkalaemia), <strong>resistance to non-depolarising NMBs</strong>, increased opioid requirements, hypermetabolism, dressing changes (ketamine, sedation).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"poisoning-principles\">Poisoning (principles)</h2>\n<p>ABC, supportive care, decontamination (activated charcoal within 1 h for suitable drugs), enhanced elimination (haemodialysis for some), antidotes; use <strong>TOXBASE</strong> and the National Poisons Information Service.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Poison</th><th>Key features</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paracetamol</td><td>Delayed hepatotoxicity</td><td><strong>Acetylcysteine</strong> per nomogram / timing</td></tr><tr><td>Aspirin (salicylate)</td><td>Respiratory alkalosis + metabolic acidosis, tinnitus, hyperthermia</td><td>Bicarbonate (urinary alkalinisation), haemodialysis</td></tr><tr><td>Opioids</td><td>Coma, miosis, respiratory depression</td><td><strong>Naloxone</strong> (titrate; infusion for long-acting drugs)</td></tr><tr><td>Tricyclics</td><td>Anticholinergic, broad QRS, seizures, arrhythmias</td><td><strong>Sodium bicarbonate</strong>, avoid Class Ia/Ic drugs, benzodiazepines</td></tr><tr><td>Digoxin</td><td>Arrhythmias, hyperkalaemia</td><td>Digoxin-specific antibody fragments</td></tr><tr><td>Theophylline (aminophylline)</td><td>Arrhythmias, seizures, hypokalaemia</td><td>Supportive, charcoal, haemodialysis</td></tr><tr><td>MDMA / stimulants</td><td>Hyperthermia, hyponatraemia, serotonin syndrome</td><td>Cooling, benzodiazepines</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol, benzodiazepines</td><td>Coma, airway</td><td>Supportive (flumazenil rarely)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"environmental-injuries\">Environmental injuries</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Drowning:</strong> hypoxia is the key problem; ventilation with PEEP; hypothermia; cervical spine only if trauma is suspected.</li><li><strong>Accidental hypothermia:</strong> stages by core temperature; J (Osborn) waves on ECG; arrhythmias; \"not dead until warm and dead\"; rewarming (ECMO for arrest); drugs withheld below 30°C in arrest.</li><li><strong>Electrical injury:</strong> arrhythmias, deep tissue damage greater than the skin burn suggests (rhabdomyolysis, compartment syndrome), myoglobinuria.</li><li><strong>Crush injury:</strong> hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias on release, rhabdomyolysis and AKI.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Parkland calculations; CO and cyanide; antidotes; suxamethonium timing in burns.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient from a house fire with a hoarse voice and facial burns: plan the airway.</li></ul></div>","cardiac-monitoring-devices":"<h2 id=\"cardiac-output-and-goal-directed-therapy\">Cardiac output and goal-directed therapy</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Devices:</strong> calibrated pulse contour (PiCCO: transpulmonary thermodilution; LiDCO: lithium), uncalibrated pulse contour, <strong>oesophageal Doppler</strong>, and the pulmonary artery catheter (thermodilution, pulmonary artery pressures, mixed venous saturation). See the Primary note on measuring cardiac output.</li><li><strong>Goal-directed therapy (GDT):</strong> protocolised fluid and inotrope administration to optimise stroke volume or oxygen delivery (e.g. giving 250 mL boluses until stroke volume no longer rises by 10%). Evidence of benefit is mixed in modern enhanced recovery pathways but it can help in high-risk surgery.</li><li><strong>Dynamic indices:</strong> stroke volume and pulse pressure variation predict fluid responsiveness in patients who are ventilated with tidal volumes ≥ 8 mL/kg, in sinus rhythm, with a closed chest.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"transoesophageal-echocardiography-toe\">Transoesophageal echocardiography (TOE)</h2>\n<p>Uses in theatre: cardiac surgery (valve assessment, de-airing, ventricular function), unexplained haemodynamic instability (<strong>rescue TOE</strong>: hypovolaemia, LV/RV failure, tamponade, dynamic LVOT obstruction, PE), and guiding structural procedures. Contraindications: oesophageal disease (strictures, varices, recent surgery). Complications: oropharyngeal and oesophageal injury.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pacing\">Pacing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Indications for temporary pacing:</strong> symptomatic bradycardia unresponsive to drugs, complete heart block, Mobitz II, some drug toxicities, after cardiac surgery.</li><li><strong>Advanced functions:</strong> rate response, cardiac resynchronisation therapy (biventricular pacing for heart failure with LBBB), ICDs (antitachycardia pacing and shocks), leadless pacemakers.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrophysiology-procedures\">Electrophysiology procedures</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>AF ablation</strong> (often under GA to keep the patient still and to control ventilation; heparinised; risk of tamponade, oesophageal injury, stroke) and <strong>VT ablation</strong> (sick patients with poor LV function; arrhythmia induction; may need haemodynamic support).</li><li><strong>ICD insertion and testing</strong> (defibrillation threshold testing is less common now): deep sedation or GA during induced VF.</li><li>Remote location, limited access to the patient, fluoroscopy, long procedures and anticoagulation.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> limitations of dynamic indices; rescue TOE findings; CRT.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> hypotension after induction for an AF ablation: work through the causes with the cardiologist.</li></ul></div>","cardiac-risk-assessment":"<h2 id=\"ischaemic-heart-disease\">Ischaemic heart disease</h2>\n<ul><li>Assess symptoms and functional capacity; ECG; further testing (stress imaging, angiography) only when it would change management independent of surgery (ESC 2022).</li><li><strong>Coronary stents:</strong> elective surgery is usually delayed until <strong>dual antiplatelet therapy</strong> (DAPT) can safely be reduced: typically <strong>6 months after an elective PCI</strong> and <strong>12 months after an acute coronary syndrome</strong>, though shorter times may be acceptable with newer stents after cardiology discussion. Continue aspirin where possible. Premature DAPT cessation risks <strong>stent thrombosis</strong> (high mortality).</li><li>Continue β-blockers and statins; do not start β-blockers just before surgery; aim to avoid tachycardia and hypotension.</li><li><strong>Perioperative myocardial injury (MINS):</strong> a troponin rise, often silent, associated with increased mortality; troponin monitoring in high-risk patients is recommended in some guidelines.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"heart-failure-and-cardiomyopathy\">Heart failure and cardiomyopathy</h2>\n<ul><li>Decompensated heart failure: defer elective surgery. Optimise medical therapy (see cardiac drugs); careful fluid management; invasive monitoring for major surgery; anticipate hypotension with ACEi/ARNI.</li><li><strong>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong> with LVOT obstruction: avoid tachycardia, hypovolaemia, vasodilation and inotropes (which worsen obstruction); phenylephrine and fluids for hypotension; β-blockers.</li><li><strong>Implanted devices:</strong> see pacemakers and ICDs.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pulmonary-hypertension\">Pulmonary hypertension</h2>\n<ul><li>High perioperative risk (RV failure). Avoid increases in <strong>PVR</strong> (hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, pain, high airway pressures, hypothermia) and falls in <strong>systemic pressure</strong> (RV coronary perfusion depends on aortic pressure); maintain RV preload and contractility. Continue pulmonary vasodilators; have inhaled NO or prostacyclin available; specialist centre input.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"adult-congenital-heart-disease\">Adult congenital heart disease</h2>\n<p>Understand the anatomy and repair (e.g. <strong>Fontan</strong>: passive pulmonary blood flow — avoid high intrathoracic pressures, maintain preload; <strong>Eisenmenger</strong>: fixed pulmonary hypertension with right-to-left shunt — avoid falls in SVR and increases in PVR; air filters on lines). Arrhythmias, residual lesions, endocarditis risk. Involve an ACHD specialist.</p>\n<h2 id=\"common-perioperative-drugs-to-review\">Common perioperative drugs to review</h2>\n<p>Anticoagulants and antiplatelets (bleeding versus thrombosis), antihypertensives (ACEi/ARB on the day), diuretics (volume and electrolytes), SGLT2 inhibitors (ketoacidosis), and hypoglycaemics.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> DAPT timing; HOCM haemodynamic goals; PH physiology; Fontan principles.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient with a drug-eluting stent inserted 4 months ago needs urgent cancer surgery: discuss with the cardiologist and surgeon.</li></ul></div>","cardiac-surgery-cpb":"<h2 id=\"the-bypass-circuit\">The bypass circuit</h2>\n<p>Venous blood drains (gravity or vacuum-assisted) from the right atrium or cavae to a <strong>reservoir</strong>, through a <strong>membrane oxygenator</strong> (gas exchange; the sweep gas flow controls CO<sub>2</sub>) and <strong>heat exchanger</strong>, and is pumped (roller or centrifugal) back through an <strong>arterial filter</strong> into the ascending aorta. Flows about <strong>2.2–2.4 L/min/m<sup>2</sup></strong> at normothermia, with a MAP of about 50–80 mmHg.</p>\n<h2 id=\"anticoagulation\">Anticoagulation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Unfractionated heparin</strong> about <strong>300–400 units/kg</strong>, aiming for an <strong>activated clotting time (ACT) &gt; 400–480 s</strong> before bypass. Heparin resistance (antithrombin deficiency) → more heparin, antithrombin concentrate or FFP.</li><li><strong>Reversal:</strong> <strong>protamine</strong> (about 1 mg per 100 units of heparin) after bypass; reactions include hypotension (rapid injection), anaphylaxis and catastrophic pulmonary hypertension.</li><li><strong>HIT</strong> patients need alternatives (e.g. bivalirudin).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"myocardial-protection-and-temperature\">Myocardial protection and temperature</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Cardioplegia</strong> (high-potassium solutions, cold or warm blood, antegrade via the aortic root or retrograde via the coronary sinus) arrests the heart in diastole, reducing oxygen demand.</li><li><strong>Hypothermia</strong> reduces metabolic rate (about 7% per °C); deep hypothermic circulatory arrest for aortic arch surgery (with cerebral protection strategies). Rewarm slowly to avoid cerebral hyperthermia and bubble formation. <strong>Alpha-stat</strong> acid–base management in adults.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"physiological-effects-of-bypass\">Physiological effects of bypass</h2>\n<p>Haemodilution, systemic inflammatory response, platelet dysfunction and consumption of coagulation factors, altered drug pharmacokinetics (dilution, sequestration in the circuit, hypothermia), microemboli (neurocognitive dysfunction and stroke), renal injury, and vasoplegia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"weaning-from-bypass\">Weaning from bypass</h2>\n<p>Checklist: temperature, rhythm (pacing wires), potassium and calcium, acid–base, Hb, <strong>ventilation restarted</strong>, de-airing (TOE), adequate contractility (inotropes as needed), filling guided by TOE. Mechanical support if needed: <strong>intra-aortic balloon pump</strong> (inflates in diastole to augment coronary perfusion, deflates before systole to reduce afterload; timed to the ECG or arterial trace; contraindicated in significant aortic regurgitation and dissection), ventricular assist devices, ECMO.</p>\n<h2 id=\"off-pump-coronary-artery-bypass\">Off-pump coronary artery bypass</h2>\n<p>Avoids bypass but requires the heart to be displaced and stabilised while beating → haemodynamic instability (head-down tilt, fluids, vasopressors, pacing), ischaemia during grafting, and a need for rapid conversion to bypass.</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-cardiac-surgery\">After cardiac surgery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Bleeding:</strong> surgical versus coagulopathic (viscoelastic testing guides products; protamine, tranexamic acid, fibrinogen, platelets).</li><li><strong>Cardiac tamponade:</strong> falling cardiac output, rising and equalising filling pressures, hypotension, reduced drain output (clotted drains), pulsus paradoxus; echocardiography helps but can be misleading after surgery (localised clot). Treatment: <strong>urgent re-sternotomy</strong> (on the unit if arrest), fluids and inotropes while preparing; avoid excess PEEP and vasodilators.</li><li>Arrhythmias (AF common), low cardiac output state, vasoplegia, AKI, stroke and delirium, respiratory failure.</li><li><strong>Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery:</strong> modified ALS (up to three quick shocks for VF, early re-sternotomy within 5 minutes, pacing for asystole) — the CALS approach.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ACT targets; protamine dosing and reactions; IABP timing; tamponade signs.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> hypotension on ICU 2 hours after CABG with falling drain output — assess and manage.</li></ul></div>","carotid-surgery":"<h2 id=\"indications-and-timing\">Indications and timing</h2>\n<p>Symptomatic carotid stenosis (TIA or minor stroke) with significant stenosis on the relevant side; surgery is most beneficial when performed <strong>early (within about 2 weeks)</strong> of symptoms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"goals\">Goals</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Maintain cerebral perfusion</strong> (especially during clamping): keep blood pressure at or slightly above baseline; normocapnia (hypocapnia vasoconstricts; hypercapnia may cause steal).</li><li><strong>Myocardial protection:</strong> these patients have a high prevalence of coronary disease.</li><li><strong>Rapid emergence</strong> for neurological assessment; avoid coughing and hypertension.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"regional-versus-general-anaesthesia\">Regional versus general anaesthesia</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Regional (awake):</strong> <strong>superficial and/or intermediate cervical plexus block</strong> (the deep block is used less because of risks: phrenic nerve palsy, vertebral artery injection, intrathecal spread) plus surgical infiltration. Allows <strong>direct neurological monitoring</strong> of the awake patient during clamping (speech, contralateral hand grip), a guide to selective shunting. Needs a cooperative patient who can lie still; conversion to GA may be needed.</li><li><strong>General:</strong> a smooth induction, invasive BP, cerebral monitoring (stump pressure, near-infrared spectroscopy, transcranial Doppler, EEG) for shunt decisions.</li><li>The <strong>GALA trial</strong> found no difference in stroke, MI or death between regional and general anaesthesia; choice is individualised.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"complications\">Complications</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Stroke</strong> (embolic or haemodynamic), MI.</li><li><strong>Haemodynamic instability:</strong> baroreceptor manipulation causes bradycardia and hypotension (local anaesthetic to the carotid sinus); denervation can cause labile BP postoperatively.</li><li><strong>Neck haematoma:</strong> can compress the airway rapidly — <strong>open the wound</strong> (remove sutures) and secure the airway early with experienced help.</li><li><strong>Cerebral hyperperfusion syndrome</strong> (days later): headache, seizures, intracerebral haemorrhage — strict blood pressure control.</li><li><strong>Cranial nerve injuries:</strong> hypoglossal, recurrent laryngeal, marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> cervical plexus block anatomy; methods of cerebral monitoring; GALA findings.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient develops neck swelling and stridor in recovery after CEA: act.</li></ul></div>","chest-wall-blocks":"<h2 id=\"chest-wall\">Chest wall</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Target</th><th>Uses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Thoracic paravertebral (PVB)</strong></td><td>Paravertebral space lateral to the vertebral body (spinal nerves as they exit)</td><td>Thoracotomy, VATS, breast surgery, rib fractures; unilateral somatic and sympathetic block; pneumothorax risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Erector spinae plane (ESP)</strong></td><td>Deep to erector spinae, on the transverse process</td><td>Rib fractures, thoracic and spinal surgery; simple, low-risk; variable spread</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Serratus anterior plane (SAP)</strong></td><td>Superficial or deep to serratus anterior (lateral cutaneous branches)</td><td>Rib fractures (lateral), VATS, breast surgery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PECS I / II</strong></td><td>Between pectoralis major and minor (± serratus)</td><td>Breast surgery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thoracic epidural</strong></td><td>Epidural space</td><td>Bilateral analgesia; hypotension, urinary retention, motor block; contraindicated with coagulopathy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Rib fractures:</strong> multimodal analgesia; regional techniques (epidural, PVB, ESP or SAP catheters) for multiple fractures or older patients, improving respiratory function and possibly outcomes; scoring tools to identify high risk.</p>\n<h2 id=\"abdominal-wall\">Abdominal wall</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Target</th><th>Coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>TAP (lateral / subcostal)</strong></td><td>Between internal oblique and transversus abdominis</td><td>Somatic abdominal wall (T7–L1 depending on approach); <strong>no visceral analgesia</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rectus sheath</strong></td><td>Posterior rectus sheath (terminal branches of T7–T11)</td><td>Midline incisions (bilateral, catheters common)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quadratus lumborum</strong></td><td>Around quadratus lumborum (several approaches)</td><td>Possibly some visceral spread (paravertebral)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ilioinguinal / iliohypogastric</strong></td><td>Between internal oblique and transversus, medial to the ASIS</td><td>Inguinal hernia, orchidopexy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Penile block</strong></td><td>Dorsal nerves of the penis (subpubic space)</td><td>Circumcision</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pudendal</strong></td><td>S2–4</td><td>Perineal surgery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Caudal</strong></td><td>Epidural via the sacral hiatus</td><td>Children (see paediatrics)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Fascial plane blocks depend on <strong>volume and spread</strong>; watch total local anaesthetic dose (bilateral catheters), and use them as part of multimodal analgesia rather than stand-alone techniques.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> layers for each block; somatic versus visceral coverage; rib fracture options.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> an elderly patient with four rib fractures and falling SpO<sub>2</sub>: explain your analgesia plan to the trauma team.</li></ul></div>","chronic-pain":"<h2 id=\"definitions\">Definitions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Chronic pain:</strong> pain lasting longer than 3 months. <strong>Chronic primary pain</strong> (e.g. fibromyalgia, chronic widespread pain, complex regional pain syndrome, chronic primary low back pain) is a condition in its own right; <strong>chronic secondary pain</strong> is due to another condition (e.g. osteoarthritis, cancer, post-surgical, neuropathic).</li><li><strong>Mechanisms:</strong> <strong>nociceptive</strong> (tissue damage; somatic or visceral), <strong>neuropathic</strong> (lesion or disease of the somatosensory nervous system), <strong>nociplastic</strong> (altered nociception without clear tissue or nerve damage — central sensitisation).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"assessment\">Assessment</h2>\n<p>History (site, onset, character, radiation, severity, timing, aggravating and relieving factors), <strong>impact on function, sleep, mood, work and relationships</strong>, beliefs and expectations, previous treatments, and <strong>red flags</strong> (e.g. cauda equina, cancer, infection, fracture). Examination for sensory changes (allodynia, hyperalgesia). Validated questionnaires (e.g. Brief Pain Inventory; DN4 or LANSS for neuropathic features).</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-biopsychosocial-model\">The biopsychosocial model</h2>\n<p>Pain experience is shaped by biology, psychology (fear-avoidance, catastrophising, depression, anxiety, trauma including <strong>adverse childhood experiences</strong>) and social factors (work, family, culture, finances). Genetic and cultural differences influence perception and reporting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"management-nice-ng193-for-chronic-primary-pain\">Management (NICE NG193 for chronic primary pain)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Supported self-management, exercise and physical activity</strong>, psychological therapies (<strong>CBT, ACT</strong>), acupuncture (in some cases), and <strong>pain management programmes</strong> delivered by a multidisciplinary team (physicians, psychologists, physiotherapists, occupational therapists, nurses).</li><li><strong>Medication:</strong> for chronic primary pain NICE recommends <strong>antidepressants</strong> (e.g. amitriptyline, duloxetine, SSRIs) may be considered; it advises <strong>against</strong> starting opioids, gabapentinoids, NSAIDs, paracetamol, benzodiazepines or local anaesthetics for chronic primary pain. For neuropathic pain, see the separate note.</li><li><strong>Barriers:</strong> patient beliefs, societal and cultural factors, access to services, and health system organisation.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> definitions; mechanisms; NICE recommendations.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a consultation with a patient with chronic low back pain requesting stronger opioids.</li></ul></div>","complex-airway-ati":"<h2 id=\"assessment\">Assessment</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>History:</strong> voice change, stridor (at rest suggests severe narrowing), dyspnoea lying flat, dysphagia, previous radiotherapy or surgery, previous anaesthetic charts (grade, devices, difficulty).</li><li><strong>Examination:</strong> mouth opening, neck movement and tissue (radiotherapy fibrosis), trismus, tongue and floor of mouth, trachea position.</li><li><strong>Investigations:</strong> <strong>flexible nasendoscopy</strong> (often with the ENT surgeon, in clinic or the anaesthetic room) showing supraglottic and glottic pathology; <strong>CT/MRI</strong> (level, extent and length of narrowing, tracheal compression, distal airway); 3D reconstruction and virtual endoscopy; flow–volume loops for fixed or variable obstruction.</li><li><strong>Shared decision-making</strong> with the surgeon and patient: which route (awake or asleep; oral, nasal, front-of-neck), which device, and the backup plans (including awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"awake-tracheal-intubation-ati\">Awake tracheal intubation (ATI)</h2>\n<p><strong>Indications:</strong> predicted difficult laryngoscopy <strong>and</strong> difficult rescue (mask, SGA or front-of-neck), significant risk of aspiration or rapid desaturation with a predicted difficult airway, limited mouth opening, unstable neck.</p>\n<p>The DAS guideline for ATI (2019) uses the elements <strong>sTOP</strong>: <strong>s</strong>edation (optional, minimal), <strong>T</strong>opicalisation, <strong>O</strong>xygenation, <strong>P</strong>erformance:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygenation:</strong> high-flow nasal oxygen throughout.</li><li><strong>Topicalisation:</strong> lidocaine to the airway (e.g. atomised or \"spray-as-you-go\" through the scope), within a safe total dose (commonly quoted maximum about 9 mg/kg lean body weight for topical lidocaine); check adequacy before instrumentation. Nerve blocks are an alternative.</li><li><strong>Sedation:</strong> optional and minimal (e.g. remifentanil TCI or small doses of midazolam), with a sedationist separate from the operator where possible; the patient must remain cooperative and breathing.</li><li><strong>Performance:</strong> flexible bronchoscope (oral or nasal) or <strong>video laryngoscope</strong>; railroad a suitably small, soft-tipped tube; confirm with capnography and bronchoscopic view <strong>before</strong> inducing anaesthesia.</li><li><strong>Limit attempts</strong> (the DAS ATI guideline suggests a maximum of three attempts, plus one by a more experienced operator) and have a clear plan if ATI fails (e.g. awake front-of-neck access).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"asleep-techniques-in-complex-airways\">Asleep techniques in complex airways</h2>\n<p>Inhalational induction maintaining spontaneous ventilation (less favoured now for many adult obstructions), IV induction with a double set-up for FONA, HFNO/apnoeic oxygenation, and jet ventilation (above or below the lesion) in selected laryngeal surgery.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> indications for ATI; lidocaine dosing; interpretation of airway imaging and nasendoscopy.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> plan an airway for a patient with a laryngeal tumour and stridor, discuss options with the surgeon and consent the patient for ATI.</li></ul></div>","cpet-risk":"<h2 id=\"risk-scoring-systems\">Risk-scoring systems</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Predicts</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ASA</td><td>General fitness</td><td>Simple, subjective, not a risk calculator</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RCRI (Lee)</strong></td><td>Major cardiac complications after non-cardiac surgery</td><td>Six factors; underestimates risk in vascular surgery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>P-POSSUM</strong></td><td>Mortality and morbidity</td><td>Needs intraoperative variables; overpredicts in low-risk patients</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SORT</strong></td><td>30-day mortality</td><td>Preoperative variables only (UK-derived)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NELA score</strong></td><td>Emergency laparotomy mortality</td><td>Used for NELA standards</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ACS NSQIP</strong> calculator</td><td>Procedure-specific outcomes</td><td>US-derived</td></tr><tr><td>Frailty scores (CFS)</td><td>Adverse outcomes in older patients</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Limitations: population estimates applied to individuals, calibration drift, and poor capture of patient-centred outcomes (disability, quality of life). Use them to <strong>inform</strong> decisions, allocate resources (critical care, seniority), and consent — not to decide alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cardiopulmonary-exercise-testing-cpet\">Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET)</h2>\n<ul><li>Incremental exercise on a cycle ergometer with breath-by-breath gas analysis and ECG.</li><li><strong>Anaerobic threshold (AT)</strong> (the point where anaerobic metabolism supplements aerobic, detected by the V-slope method): an AT below about <strong>10–11 mL/kg/min</strong> is associated with higher perioperative risk for major abdominal surgery. <strong>Peak VO<sub>2</sub></strong> below about <strong>15 mL/kg/min</strong> likewise. Also ventilatory equivalents for CO<sub>2</sub> (VE/VCO<sub>2</sub> &gt; about 34 indicates poor ventilatory efficiency, e.g. pulmonary vascular disease), ECG ischaemia and blood pressure response.</li><li>Uses: risk stratification, identifying unexpected pathology, guiding prehabilitation and level of postoperative care, shared decision-making.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-functional-assessments\">Other functional assessments</h2>\n<p>Self-reported exercise tolerance (METs; DASI questionnaire), shuttle walk tests, six-minute walk test, BNP/NT-proBNP (ESC 2022 recommends in some patients), echocardiography for specific indications (murmurs, dyspnoea).</p>\n<h2 id=\"prehabilitation-and-optimisation\">Prehabilitation and optimisation</h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise</strong> (aerobic and resistance), <strong>nutrition</strong> (protein), <strong>psychological</strong> support, <strong>smoking and alcohol</strong> cessation, treatment of <strong>anaemia</strong> (IV iron), diabetes and hypertension optimisation. Multimodal prehabilitation over weeks can improve functional capacity; evidence for outcomes is growing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"shared-decision-making\">Shared decision-making</h2>\n<p>A collaborative process (e.g. <strong>BRAN</strong>: Benefits, Risks, Alternatives, and doing Nothing) that combines clinical evidence with what matters to the patient. Supported by decision aids, time, and multidisciplinary input (surgeon, anaesthetist, geriatrician). Particularly important for high-risk and older patients.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CPET thresholds; strengths and weaknesses of risk tools.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> explaining CPET results and the risks of surgery to a patient, and exploring what they want.</li></ul></div>","ecmo-advanced":"<h2 id=\"ecmo\">ECMO</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Veno-venous (VV)</strong></th><th><strong>Veno-arterial (VA)</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supports</td><td><strong>Gas exchange</strong> (lungs)</td><td><strong>Heart and lungs</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cannulation</td><td>Femoral and jugular veins (or a dual-lumen jugular cannula)</td><td>Femoral vein → femoral artery (peripheral) or central</td></tr><tr><td>Indications</td><td>Severe ARDS with refractory hypoxaemia or hypercapnia despite optimal ventilation and prone positioning</td><td>Cardiogenic shock (MI, myocarditis, post-cardiotomy), refractory cardiac arrest (<strong>ECPR</strong>), PE</td></tr><tr><td>Issues</td><td>Recirculation; the native heart must work</td><td>Increased LV afterload (LV distension — venting); differential hypoxia (\"Harlequin\" syndrome) with peripheral cannulation and poor lung function</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Complications:</strong> bleeding (anticoagulation), thrombosis, circuit failure, haemolysis, limb ischaemia (VA), infection, neurological events (stroke).</li><li><strong>UK model:</strong> severe respiratory failure ECMO is provided by a small number of commissioned <strong>national centres</strong> with retrieval teams; referral criteria include potentially reversible severe respiratory failure (e.g. Murray score) and a short duration of ventilation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-mechanical-support\">Other mechanical support</h2>\n<p>Intra-aortic balloon pump, percutaneous ventricular assist devices (e.g. Impella), long-term LVADs (bridge to transplant or destination therapy) — patients with LVADs have continuous flow (often no palpable pulse; use Doppler or arterial line for BP), anticoagulation and driveline infection risks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"temperature-control-after-cardiac-arrest\">Temperature control after cardiac arrest</h2>\n<p>Current guidance recommends <strong>avoiding fever</strong> (e.g. keeping temperature ≤ 37.5°C for at least 72 hours) rather than routine deep hypothermia, following trials showing no benefit of 33°C over normothermia with fever prevention. Methods: surface or intravascular devices, sedation, managing shivering.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> VV versus VA; Harlequin syndrome; post-arrest temperature targets.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> discussing referral for ECMO with a family and with the regional ECMO centre.</li></ul></div>","elderly-cognition":"<h2 id=\"frailty\">Frailty</h2>\n<ul><li>A state of reduced physiological reserve and vulnerability to stressors. Assessed with the <strong>Clinical Frailty Scale</strong> (CFS 1–9; ≥ 5 is frail), gait speed, grip strength, and comprehensive geriatric assessment.</li><li>Predicts complications, delirium, loss of independence, and mortality after surgery better than age alone. Proactive care (e.g. <strong>POPS</strong> — perioperative medicine for older people undergoing surgery — services) improves outcomes.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"drugs-in-older-people\">Drugs in older people</h2>\n<p>Reduced doses and slower titration: reduced volume of the central compartment, reduced clearance (renal and hepatic), increased brain sensitivity (lower MAC, opioid sensitivity), longer circulation times (slow onset — don't chase with more drug). Polypharmacy and interactions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"delirium\">Delirium</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and cognition</strong>; hyperactive, hypoactive (commoner and often missed) or mixed. Common after emergency and major surgery, especially hip fracture (up to about half of patients).</li><li><strong>Associated with</strong> increased mortality, length of stay, institutionalisation and long-term cognitive decline.</li><li><strong>Risk factors:</strong> age, dementia, frailty, sensory impairment, depression, alcohol, polypharmacy, severity of illness, emergency surgery.</li><li><strong>Prevention (multicomponent):</strong> orientation, glasses and hearing aids, sleep, hydration and nutrition, early mobilisation, adequate analgesia, avoid <strong>anticholinergics and benzodiazepines</strong>, minimise catheters and restraints, treat infection and constipation; possibly processed EEG to avoid excessive anaesthetic depth.</li><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> 4AT (ward), CAM-ICU (ICU). <strong>Management:</strong> treat causes, non-drug measures; low-dose antipsychotics only for severe distress or danger (avoid in Parkinson's and Lewy body dementia).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"postoperative-cognitive-dysfunction-and-neurocognitive-disorders\">Postoperative cognitive dysfunction and neurocognitive disorders</h2>\n<p>The term \"<strong>perioperative neurocognitive disorders</strong>\" includes delirium, delayed neurocognitive recovery (to 30 days) and postoperative neurocognitive disorder (up to 12 months). Risk rises with age, pre-existing impairment, major surgery and complications. The anaesthetic technique (GA versus regional) has not been shown to make a consistent difference.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dementia-and-reasonable-adjustments\">Dementia and reasonable adjustments</h2>\n<p>Involve carers (hospital passport, \"This is me\"), familiar people present at induction and in recovery, continuity, a calm environment, capacity assessment and best interests decisions, and pain assessment tools for non-verbal patients (PAINAD, Abbey).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CFS; delirium risk factors and prevention; drugs to avoid.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a conversation with the daughter of a patient with dementia who became delirious after surgery.</li></ul></div>","emergency-laparotomy":"<h2 id=\"why-it-matters\">Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Emergency laparotomy carries a high mortality (around 10% at 30 days in national data, much higher in older and frail patients). The <strong>National Emergency Laparotomy Audit (NELA)</strong> has driven improvements through standards of care.</p>\n<h2 id=\"key-standards-nela-derived\">Key standards (NELA-derived)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Documented risk assessment</strong> before surgery (the <strong>NELA risk calculator</strong>; P-POSSUM) to guide the level of seniority and postoperative care.</li><li><strong>Consultant surgeon and consultant anaesthetist present</strong> in theatre for high-risk patients (predicted mortality ≥ 5%).</li><li><strong>Timely surgery</strong> matching the urgency (e.g. immediate for septic shock with peritonitis) and <strong>antibiotics within 1 hour</strong> of suspected sepsis.</li><li><strong>Admission to critical care</strong> for high-risk patients.</li><li><strong>Elderly care (geriatrician) review</strong> for older patients (e.g. over 65 or frail).</li><li>CT reported by a consultant radiologist before surgery.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"anaesthetic-conduct\">Anaesthetic conduct</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Resuscitation in parallel</strong> with preparation (fluids, vasopressors, blood), arterial line before induction in unstable patients.</li><li><strong>RSI</strong> (aspiration risk, bowel obstruction; empty the stomach with a nasogastric tube) with <strong>reduced-dose induction</strong>, vasopressor running or drawn up.</li><li><strong>Goal-directed haemodynamic management</strong>, lung-protective ventilation, temperature control, glucose control, blood products as needed.</li><li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> opioids, rectus sheath or TAP catheters, wound infiltration, epidural in selected patients without sepsis or coagulopathy.</li><li><strong>End of surgery:</strong> decide on extubation versus ventilation, and the destination (ICU or enhanced care).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"shared-decision-making-and-ceilings-of-care\">Shared decision-making and ceilings of care</h2>\n<p>For frail or very high-risk patients, discuss with the patient (or those important to them) the likely outcomes, including <strong>not operating</strong> and palliative care. Record treatment escalation plans and ceilings of treatment; respect the patient's values.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> NELA standards; risk tools; timing of antibiotics.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a conversation with an 88-year-old's family about the risks of emergency laparotomy and the option not to operate.</li></ul></div>","end-of-life-donation":"<h2 id=\"communication-and-decisions\">Communication and decisions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Discharge from ICU:</strong> timely (avoid night-time discharges), clear written and verbal handover, rehabilitation plans, follow-up clinics (post-intensive care syndrome).</li><li><strong>Treatment escalation plans and ceilings of care:</strong> discussed with the patient where possible, or those important to them, based on likely benefit and the patient's values; decisions made by the senior clinician; documented clearly (e.g. ReSPECT).</li><li><strong>Breaking bad news:</strong> prepare (setting, people, information), check what they know, give a warning shot, deliver clearly and compassionately, allow silence, respond to emotion, summarise the plan (SPIKES framework). Be aware of cultural and religious differences.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"withdrawing-life-sustaining-treatment\">Withdrawing life-sustaining treatment</h2>\n<p>There is no ethical or legal difference between withholding and withdrawing treatment that is no longer in the patient's best interests. Focus on <strong>comfort</strong>: symptom control (opioids, anxiolytics, anti-secretory drugs) with doses titrated to effect (the principle of double effect), family presence, spiritual care, and stopping non-beneficial interventions (monitoring alarms, blood tests). Palliative care teams support end-of-life care in ICU and for patients with end-stage disease.</p>\n<h2 id=\"brainstem-death-and-donation-after-brainstem-death-dbd\">Brainstem death and donation after brainstem death (DBD)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Preconditions and tests</strong> as in the Primary note (Academy of Medical Royal Colleges code); two sets of tests; death certified after the first set.</li><li><strong>Physiological support of the donor:</strong> loss of brainstem function causes cardiovascular instability (initial catecholamine storm, then vasodilation), <strong>diabetes insipidus</strong>, hypothermia, coagulopathy, and endocrine failure. Targets and management (vasopressin, fluids, hormone therapy per protocols) optimise organ function for transplantation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"donation-after-circulatory-death-dcd\">Donation after circulatory death (DCD)</h2>\n<p>After a decision to withdraw treatment, with consent, death is confirmed after circulatory arrest and a 5-minute observation period; organs are retrieved promptly. Normothermic regional perfusion is used in some centres.</p>\n<h2 id=\"legal-and-ethical-framework\">Legal and ethical framework</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Consent:</strong> deemed consent (\"opt-out\") legislation in England, Wales and Scotland, with the family's views remaining central in practice; the Organ Donor Register; the <strong>specialist nurse for organ donation</strong> should be involved early in every potential donor.</li><li>Coroner or procurator fiscal involvement when required; the ethics of living donation (donor safety, freedom from coercion); cultural and religious considerations.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> DBD physiology; DCD process; legal framework.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> telling a family that their son has died on brainstem testing, and raising organ donation with the specialist nurse.</li></ul></div>","endocrine-disease-pom":"<h2 id=\"diabetes-stage-2-emphasis\">Diabetes (Stage 2 emphasis)</h2>\n<p>Complications that matter: cardiovascular disease (silent ischaemia), nephropathy, <strong>autonomic neuropathy</strong> (gastroparesis → aspiration; labile BP; painless ischaemia), stiff joints (difficult laryngoscopy), infection and poor healing. See the Primary diabetes notes for perioperative glucose management.</p>\n<h2 id=\"thyroid\">Thyroid</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism:</strong> make euthyroid before elective surgery (carbimazole or PTU, β-blockade); <strong>thyroid storm</strong> (fever, tachycardia, AF, agitation, heart failure) — β-blockers, antithyroid drugs, iodide, hydrocortisone, cooling, supportive care.</li><li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> sensitivity to anaesthetics, hypothermia, hyponatraemia, hypoventilation; <strong>myxoedema coma</strong> (a medical emergency).</li><li><strong>Thyroid surgery:</strong> goitre with <strong>tracheal compression or retrosternal extension</strong> (CT; flow–volume loop; plan awake or asleep techniques; rarely need cardiopulmonary bypass standby), recurrent laryngeal nerve monitoring (NIM tubes: avoid long-acting NMB), postoperative haematoma (open the wound), hypocalcaemia, tracheomalacia (rare).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"adrenal\">Adrenal</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Adrenal insufficiency</strong> (primary or secondary including exogenous steroids): perioperative steroid cover (2020 guideline); crisis presents with refractory hypotension → hydrocortisone.</li><li><strong>Cushing's syndrome:</strong> hypertension, diabetes, obesity, OSA, fragile skin and veins, osteoporosis, poor healing, hypokalaemia.</li><li><strong>Conn's syndrome</strong> (hyperaldosteronism): hypertension, hypokalaemia (correct before surgery; spironolactone).</li><li><strong>Phaeochromocytoma:</strong> see the separate note.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pituitary\">Pituitary</h2>\n<p><strong>Acromegaly</strong> (difficult airway: macroglossia, prognathism, laryngeal narrowing; OSA; cardiomyopathy; diabetes), <strong>Cushing's disease</strong>, prolactinoma, and <strong>hypopituitarism</strong> (steroid and thyroid replacement; DI after surgery).</p>\n<h2 id=\"endocrine-emergencies-in-critical-care\">Endocrine emergencies in critical care</h2>\n<p>DKA and HHS (see diabetes), <strong>diabetes insipidus</strong> (after head injury or brainstem death: polyuria, hypernatraemia — desmopressin), <strong>SIADH</strong>, thyroid storm, myxoedema coma, adrenal crisis, hypercalcaemia (malignancy; fluids, bisphosphonates), and hypoglycaemia.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> thyroid storm treatment; steroid cover; acromegaly airway issues.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient with a large retrosternal goitre and orthopnoea for thyroidectomy: plan with the surgeon.</li></ul></div>","ent-laser-jet":"<h2 id=\"surgery-on-or-below-the-vocal-cords\">Surgery on or below the vocal cords</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Features</th><th>Concerns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Microlaryngeal tube</strong> (e.g. 5.0–6.0 mm ID)</td><td>Secure airway, cuffed, capnography</td><td>Obscures the posterior glottis</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jet ventilation</strong> (supraglottic, subglottic or transtracheal; manual or high-frequency)</td><td>Tubeless, good surgical view</td><td><strong>Barotrauma and pneumothorax</strong> if outflow is obstructed; no end-tidal monitoring; aspiration; needs TIVA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HFNO/apnoeic oxygenation (e.g. THRIVE)</strong></td><td>Tubeless for short cases</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub> rises (about 0.15–0.2 kPa/min) — limits duration; not with laser (FiO<sub>2</sub> high)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermittent apnoea</td><td>Remove tube, operate, re-intubate</td><td>Repeated instrumentation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Laser surgery:</strong> laser-resistant tubes with saline-filled cuffs, FiO<sub>2</sub> ≤ 0.3, no N<sub>2</sub>O, eye protection, an airway fire plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"major-head-and-neck-cancer-surgery\">Major head and neck cancer surgery</h2>\n<ul><li>Patients often have <strong>smoking and alcohol</strong>-related disease (COPD, IHD, malnutrition, alcohol withdrawal), previous radiotherapy (fibrosis, difficult airway), and tumours that distort the airway.</li><li>Airway plan (ATI, elective tracheostomy), long procedures (temperature, pressure areas, fluid balance), <strong>free flap</strong> considerations (see free flap surgery), and postoperative critical care.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"middle-ear-and-nasal-surgery\">Middle ear and nasal surgery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Middle ear:</strong> a bloodless field (moderate controlled hypotension, head-up tilt, <strong>TIVA with remifentanil</strong> is popular), <strong>avoid N<sub>2</sub>O</strong> (pressure changes in the middle ear displace grafts), facial nerve monitoring (avoid neuromuscular block after intubation), PONV prophylaxis (high risk).</li><li><strong>Nasal and sinus surgery:</strong> vasoconstrictors (cocaine, adrenaline, xylometazoline — watch for hypertension and arrhythmias), throat packs, reinforced LMA or oral RAE tube, smooth emergence.</li><li><strong>Supraglottic airways for IPPV or SV:</strong> second-generation SGAs are widely used for ENT with surgical agreement; limitations include displacement by gags and leaks.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> jet ventilation hazards; laser safety; why N<sub>2</sub>O is avoided in middle ear surgery.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> discussing airway options for microlaryngoscopy with the surgeon, and managing a sudden rise in airway pressure during jet ventilation.</li></ul></div>","haematology-pom":"<h2 id=\"sickle-cell-disease\">Sickle cell disease</h2>\n<ul><li>HbSS (sickle cell anaemia) and compound states (HbSC). Chronic haemolysis, vaso-occlusive crises, acute chest syndrome, stroke, splenic infarction, renal and pulmonary complications.</li><li><strong>Perioperative care:</strong> involve haematology; <strong>preoperative transfusion</strong> to Hb about 100 g/L for medium- and high-risk surgery in HbSS (per evidence from trials); avoid <strong>hypoxia, dehydration, hypothermia, acidosis and stasis</strong> (tourniquets are relatively contraindicated); good analgesia; incentive spirometry and early mobilisation to prevent <strong>acute chest syndrome</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sickle cell trait</strong> is generally not a major anaesthetic problem.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"bleeding-and-clotting-disorders\">Bleeding and clotting disorders</h2>\n<p>Haemophilia (factor replacement with haematology), von Willebrand disease (desmopressin or concentrates), thrombocytopenia (ITP, drugs), <strong>thrombophilias</strong> (VTE prophylaxis), <strong>antiphospholipid syndrome</strong> (thrombosis despite prolonged APTT in vitro), heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cancer-therapy\">Cancer therapy</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Chemotherapy:</strong> cardiotoxicity (<strong>anthracyclines</strong> such as doxorubicin: cardiomyopathy; trastuzumab), pulmonary toxicity (<strong>bleomycin</strong>: sensitivity to high FiO<sub>2</sub> — use the lowest FiO<sub>2</sub> needed), neuropathy (vincristine, platins), nephrotoxicity (cisplatin), bone marrow suppression (infection, bleeding), mucositis.</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy:</strong> fibrosis of the neck (difficult airway and FONA), lungs and heart.</li><li><strong>Immunotherapies</strong> (checkpoint inhibitors): endocrine and other autoimmune effects (adrenal insufficiency, thyroiditis, pneumonitis).</li><li>Tumour effects: anaemia, hypercalcaemia, SVC obstruction, mediastinal masses, cachexia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"nutrition\">Nutrition</h2>\n<p>Assess with weight loss, BMI, intake and screening tools (MUST), albumin (a marker of illness rather than nutrition). Malnutrition impairs wound healing, immunity, respiratory muscle function and thermoregulation. Prehabilitation includes nutritional support; perioperative feeding (early enteral), and refeeding precautions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cultural-ethnic-and-personal-factors\">Cultural, ethnic and personal factors</h2>\n<p>Respect beliefs about blood products, animal-derived products (e.g. porcine heparin, gelatin in colloids or capsules), fasting during religious periods, and end-of-life care. <strong>Ethnicity</strong> matters for some conditions (sickle cell, G6PD deficiency, CYP2D6 variants), pulse oximetry bias with darker skin, and inequalities in outcomes (MBRRACE). Use professional interpreters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"substance-misuse-and-addiction\">Substance misuse and addiction</h2>\n<p>Opioid dependence (continue methadone or buprenorphine; higher analgesic needs; regional anaesthesia), alcohol (withdrawal prophylaxis, thiamine, liver disease), stimulants (cardiovascular risk), and the wider psychosocial context; involve addiction services.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> sickle cell precautions; bleomycin and oxygen; anthracycline cardiotoxicity.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> preassessment of a patient with sickle cell disease listed for cholecystectomy.</li></ul></div>","icm-neuro-haem-endocrine":"<h2 id=\"neurology\">Neurology</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Sedation and delirium:</strong> analgesia-first light sedation (RASS 0 to −2), daily sedation holds, CAM-ICU screening, non-drug delirium measures, early mobilisation; avoid benzodiazepine infusions where possible. Dexmedetomidine and propofol are common.</li><li><strong>Reduced consciousness:</strong> structural (stroke, haemorrhage, trauma), metabolic (glucose, sodium, hepatic, uraemic, hypercapnia), infective (meningitis, encephalitis), toxic, seizures (non-convulsive status — EEG).</li><li><strong>Neuromuscular weakness:</strong> <strong>Guillain–Barré</strong> (ascending weakness, areflexia, autonomic instability; monitor vital capacity — intubate if &lt; about 15–20 mL/kg or rapidly falling; IV immunoglobulin or plasma exchange) and <strong>myasthenic crisis</strong> (IVIG, plasma exchange, ventilatory support). ICU-acquired weakness (critical illness polyneuropathy and myopathy).</li><li><strong>Hypoxic brain injury</strong> after cardiac arrest: temperature control, avoid hypotension and hypoxia, multimodal prognostication after at least 72 hours; <strong>brainstem death</strong> testing when appropriate.</li><li><strong>Autonomic disorders:</strong> dysautonomia in GBS, spinal cord injury and tetanus.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"haematology\">Haematology</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Anaemia</strong> (restrictive transfusion), <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> (sepsis, DIC, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia — use the 4Ts score, drugs, TTP — plasma exchange), <strong>coagulopathy</strong> (DIC, liver disease, anticoagulants, dilution), <strong>haematological malignancy</strong> (neutropenic sepsis, tumour lysis syndrome, hyperviscosity), sickle cell crises (acute chest syndrome).</li><li><strong>Blood-borne viruses:</strong> hepatitis B and C, HIV — universal precautions, needlestick management and post-exposure prophylaxis, and the effects of HIV and antiretrovirals (drug interactions, e.g. ritonavir inhibits CYP3A4) on anaesthesia and ICU care.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"endocrine-emergencies\">Endocrine emergencies</h2>\n<p>DKA, HHS, diabetes insipidus and SIADH, adrenal crisis, thyroid storm and myxoedema coma, hypercalcaemia and hypocalcaemia, hypoglycaemia (see endocrine notes).</p>\n<h2 id=\"children-in-intensive-care\">Children in intensive care</h2>\n<p>Recognition and initial management of the critically ill child (sepsis, bronchiolitis, trauma, seizures, DKA), with early involvement of paediatric retrieval services (see complex paediatrics).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> GBS intubation criteria; HIT scoring; tumour lysis.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> reviewing a patient with Guillain–Barré whose vital capacity has fallen to 18 mL/kg: decide and explain.</li></ul></div>","lung-resection-assessment":"<h2 id=\"the-three-legs-of-assessment\">The three legs of assessment</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Respiratory mechanics:</strong> FEV<sub>1</sub> (and predicted postoperative FEV<sub>1</sub>, ppoFEV<sub>1</sub>).</li><li><strong>Lung parenchymal function:</strong> diffusing capacity (DLCO) and ppoDLCO; arterial blood gases.</li><li><strong>Cardiopulmonary reserve:</strong> exercise testing (stair climbing, shuttle walk, <strong>CPET</strong> with VO<sub>2</sub> max).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"predicted-postoperative-values\">Predicted postoperative values</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>ppoFEV<sub>1</sub> = preoperative FEV<sub>1</sub> × (1 − number of functional segments removed / total functional segments)</strong> (19 segments in total, or adjusted for obstructed segments; perfusion scans help for pneumonectomy).</li><li>Example: FEV<sub>1</sub> 70% predicted, right lower lobe (5 segments) removed → ppoFEV<sub>1</sub> ≈ 70 × (14/19) ≈ 52%.</li><li><strong>ppoFEV<sub>1</sub> and ppoDLCO &gt; 60% predicted</strong>: low risk. Values between about <strong>30% and 60%</strong> need exercise testing. Below about <strong>30%</strong> (either): high risk — consider limited resection or non-surgical treatment.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-testing\">Exercise testing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>VO<sub>2</sub> max &gt; 20 mL/kg/min:</strong> low risk. <strong>&lt; 10 mL/kg/min:</strong> high risk for major resection. Intermediate values need individualised decisions.</li><li>Simple tests: <strong>stair climbing</strong> (ability to climb &gt; 22 m, about 5–6 flights, suggests low risk), incremental shuttle walk (&gt; 400 m).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-factors\">Other factors</h2>\n<p>Cardiac risk (e.g. thoracic RCRI), age and frailty, smoking cessation, nutritional status, pulmonary rehabilitation (prehabilitation), and the patient's own priorities in a shared decision-making discussion.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate ppoFEV<sub>1</sub>; thresholds for further testing; VO<sub>2</sub> max cut-offs.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> preassessment discussion with a patient with COPD and a lung tumour about the risks of lobectomy.</li></ul></div>","major-abdominal-surgery":"<h2 id=\"enhanced-recovery-eras\">Enhanced recovery (ERAS)</h2>\n<p>A bundle of evidence-based elements: preoperative education, prehabilitation and optimisation (anaemia, nutrition, smoking, alcohol), minimal fasting with carbohydrate loading, <strong>minimally invasive surgery</strong>, goal-directed or balanced fluid therapy (avoiding overload), multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia, PONV prophylaxis, normothermia, avoiding drains and nasogastric tubes, <strong>early feeding and mobilisation</strong>. Reduces complications and length of stay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"liver-resection\">Liver resection</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Low central venous pressure technique</strong> during parenchymal transection (fluid restriction, head-up tilt, vasodilators) reduces hepatic venous bleeding, at the risk of air embolism and hypoperfusion; restore volume after resection.</li><li><strong>Pringle manoeuvre</strong> (clamping the portal triad) limits inflow; ischaemic tolerance limits.</li><li>Postoperative liver dysfunction (small future remnant), coagulopathy (affects epidural catheter management), and analgesia (wound catheters, spinal opioids, epidural where coagulation allows).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pancreatic-surgery-whipple-s\">Pancreatic surgery (Whipple's)</h2>\n<p>Long procedure; jaundice (coagulopathy, renal risk), diabetes, malnutrition, fluid balance, and postoperative pancreatic fistula and delayed gastric emptying.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oesophagectomy\">Oesophagectomy</h2>\n<p>Two- or three-stage (abdominal and thoracic, ± neck); <strong>one-lung ventilation</strong> for the thoracic stage; aspiration risk (achalasia-like physiology, obstruction); malnutrition; thoracic epidural or paravertebral analgesia; avoid fluid overload and hypotension (anastomotic perfusion); complications include anastomotic leak, pneumonia, ARDS, AF, chylothorax and recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"robotic-and-laparoscopic-surgery\">Robotic and laparoscopic surgery</h2>\n<p>Steep <strong>Trendelenburg</strong> (raised ICP and IOP, facial and airway oedema, reduced FRC, ventilation challenges) and pneumoperitoneum; limited access to the patient once docked (secure lines and tubes beforehand; plan for emergency undocking); positioning injuries; longer procedures.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bariatric-surgery\">Bariatric surgery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Preoperative:</strong> OSA (CPAP), obesity hypoventilation, cardiovascular disease, diabetes, a liver-reducing diet.</li><li><strong>Intraoperative:</strong> ramped positioning, pre-oxygenation with CPAP/HFNO, video laryngoscopy, lean-body-weight drug dosing, protective ventilation with PEEP and recruitment, TIVA or low-solubility agents, multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia.</li><li><strong>Postoperative:</strong> early mobilisation, CPAP for OSA, VTE prophylaxis (weight-adjusted), watch for anastomotic leak (tachycardia is an early sign).</li><li><strong>After bariatric surgery:</strong> dumping syndrome, micronutrient deficiencies (B<sub>12</sub>, iron, folate, thiamine), altered drug absorption, and aspiration risk after sleeve gastrectomy.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"depth-of-anaesthesia-monitoring\">Depth of anaesthesia monitoring</h2>\n<p><strong>Processed EEG</strong> (e.g. BIS: 40–60 is the usual target for GA) reduces the risk of awareness with TIVA and neuromuscular block and may reduce anaesthetic exposure in older patients (possibly reducing delirium). Limitations: neuromuscular activity (EMG), ketamine and N<sub>2</sub>O (not reflected), hypothermia, age, and neurological disease.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ERAS elements; low CVP technique; Trendelenburg effects; BIS limitations.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> plan the anaesthetic and analgesia for an Ivor Lewis oesophagectomy, and explain it to the patient.</li></ul></div>","neonatal-anaesthesia":"<h2 id=\"what-is-different\">What is different</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Airway:</strong> large occiput and tongue, high anterior larynx, floppy epiglottis, short trachea (easy endobronchial intubation), obligate nasal breathing in early months.</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> high oxygen consumption (6–8 mL/kg/min), low FRC, closing volume near FRC, immature ventilatory control (<strong>apnoea</strong>, especially in ex-prems), a fatigue-prone diaphragm.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> rate-dependent output, <strong>transitional circulation</strong> (reversion to fetal circulation with hypoxia, acidosis and hypothermia), limited contractile reserve.</li><li><strong>Renal and hepatic:</strong> immature concentrating ability and drug metabolism; reduced clearance of many drugs in the first weeks.</li><li><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> larger ECF (↑ Vd of water-soluble drugs), higher MAC (except in preterm neonates), sensitivity to non-depolarising NMBs varies; <strong>reduced plasma protein binding</strong>.</li><li><strong>Temperature:</strong> high surface area to volume ratio; <strong>non-shivering thermogenesis</strong> (brown fat) increases oxygen consumption.</li><li><strong>Glucose:</strong> limited glycogen stores → hypoglycaemia with prolonged fasting; give glucose-containing maintenance fluid.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"conduct-of-anaesthesia\">Conduct of anaesthesia</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Preparation:</strong> experienced team, warmed theatre (and warming mattress, overhead heater, warm fluids, humidification), correctly sized equipment, drugs by weight double-checked, and confirmation that vitamin K has been given.</li><li><strong>Induction:</strong> IV or inhalational; awake intubation is now rare; cuffed microcuff tubes are widely used. Avoid hyperoxia in preterm infants (retinopathy of prematurity) — titrate SpO<sub>2</sub> to targets.</li><li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> pre- and post-ductal SpO<sub>2</sub> where relevant, temperature, glucose, blood loss (small absolute volumes are significant).</li><li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> paracetamol (reduced dose), regional (caudal, spinal), careful opioids with monitoring.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-neonatal-surgical-problems\">Common neonatal surgical problems</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pyloric stenosis</strong> (2–8 weeks): <strong>a medical, not surgical, emergency</strong> — projectile vomiting causes <strong>hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis</strong> (with paradoxical aciduria). <strong>Resuscitate first</strong> with fluids and correct electrolytes (e.g. chloride &gt; 100 mmol/L, bicarbonate &lt; 28 mmol/L), then surgery. Empty the stomach with a nasogastric tube before induction; RSI or modified RSI; laparoscopic or open pyloromyotomy; postoperative apnoea monitoring.</li><li><strong>Inguinal hernia</strong> in ex-premature infants, intestinal obstruction (malrotation with volvulus is an emergency), necrotising enterocolitis, gastroschisis and exomphalos.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-ex-premature-infant\">The ex-premature infant</h2>\n<p><strong>Postoperative apnoea</strong> risk is highest in infants below about <strong>60 weeks post-conceptual age</strong>, especially with anaemia; they need overnight apnoea monitoring after GA. Spinal anaesthesia without sedation may reduce early apnoea. Also bronchopulmonary dysplasia, subglottic stenosis from prolonged intubation.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> physiological differences; pyloric stenosis biochemistry; apnoea risk thresholds.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> plan anaesthesia for a 5-week-old with pyloric stenosis, and explain the delay for resuscitation to the parents.</li></ul></div>","neuroanaesthesia-principles":"<h2 id=\"goals\">Goals</h2>\n<p>Maintain <strong>cerebral perfusion and oxygenation</strong>, reduce <strong>ICP</strong> and brain bulk for surgical access, avoid secondary injury, allow <strong>rapid, smooth emergence</strong> for early neurological assessment, and facilitate neuromonitoring.</p>\n<h2 id=\"effects-of-anaesthetic-drugs\">Effects of anaesthetic drugs</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>CBF</th><th>CMRO<sub>2</sub></th><th>ICP</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Propofol</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>Preserves autoregulation and coupling; TIVA popular</td></tr><tr><td>Thiopental</td><td>↓</td><td>↓↓</td><td>↓</td><td>Burst suppression for refractory ICP</td></tr><tr><td>Volatile (&lt; 1 MAC)</td><td>↑ (dose-dependent vasodilation)</td><td>↓</td><td>↔/↑</td><td>\"Uncoupling\"; sevoflurane least vasodilating; autoregulation impaired at higher doses</td></tr><tr><td>N<sub>2</sub>O</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td><td>Usually avoided</td></tr><tr><td>Ketamine</td><td>↑ (if PaCO<sub>2</sub> rises)</td><td>↑/↔</td><td>↔ with controlled ventilation</td><td>Acceptable in TBI with ventilation</td></tr><tr><td>Opioids</td><td>↔</td><td>↔</td><td>↔ (if normocapnic)</td><td>Remifentanil allows rapid emergence</td></tr><tr><td>Suxamethonium</td><td>Transient ↑ ICP</td><td></td><td></td><td>Not contraindicated when needed for RSI</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"reducing-icp-and-brain-bulk\">Reducing ICP and brain bulk</h2>\n<p>Head-up <strong>15–30°</strong>, neutral neck, loose ties; adequate depth and analgesia (avoid coughing and straining); <strong>normocapnia</strong> (brief hyperventilation to PaCO<sub>2</sub> ~4 kPa only as a rescue); normoxia; normothermia; normoglycaemia; <strong>osmotherapy</strong> (mannitol 0.25–1 g/kg or hypertonic saline); CSF drainage (external ventricular drain, lumbar drain); steroids (dexamethasone) for <strong>tumour-related vasogenic oedema</strong> only (not TBI); barbiturate coma or decompressive craniectomy for refractory ICP.</p>\n<h2 id=\"monitoring\">Monitoring</h2>\n<p>Standard monitoring plus <strong>arterial line</strong> (transducer at the tragus for CPP when head-up), sometimes CVP, ICP, jugular bulb saturation, processed EEG. <strong>Neurophysiological monitoring</strong> (SSEPs, MEPs, EMG) requires TIVA and <strong>avoidance of neuromuscular block</strong> after intubation (for MEPs); volatile agents depress evoked potentials in a dose-dependent way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-issues\">Other issues</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Positioning:</strong> prone (eyes, face, abdomen free), park bench, sitting (venous air embolism, pneumocephalus), pinned head (the pinning response needs analgesia or local anaesthetic).</li><li><strong>VTE:</strong> neurosurgical patients are at high risk, but anticoagulation risks intracranial bleeding: mechanical prophylaxis, then pharmacological at a time agreed with the neurosurgeon.</li><li><strong>Emergence:</strong> smooth, without hypertension or coughing (remifentanil, lidocaine, labetalol); early neurological examination.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> drug effects on CBF/CMRO<sub>2</sub>/ICP; osmotherapy; effects on evoked potentials.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a brain tumour craniotomy: explain your technique and how you would manage brain swelling during surgery.</li></ul></div>","neuroendocrine-tumours":"<h2 id=\"phaeochromocytoma\">Phaeochromocytoma</h2>\n<ul><li>Catecholamine-secreting tumour of the adrenal medulla (paragangliomas elsewhere); may be part of MEN 2, von Hippel–Lindau or neurofibromatosis.</li><li><strong>Features:</strong> paroxysmal or sustained hypertension, headache, sweating, palpitations, anxiety; cardiomyopathy; hyperglycaemia. Diagnosis by plasma or urinary <strong>metanephrines</strong>, then imaging.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Preoperative preparation (usually 10–14 days)</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>α-blockade first:</strong> <strong>phenoxybenzamine</strong> (non-selective, irreversible) or <strong>doxazosin</strong> (selective α<sub>1</sub>). Liberal salt and fluid intake to restore the contracted plasma volume.</li><li><strong>β-blockade only after adequate α-blockade</strong> (for tachycardia): unopposed α stimulation after β-blockade can precipitate a hypertensive crisis and heart failure.</li><li><strong>Roizen's criteria</strong> (targets such as BP &lt; 160/90 for 24 h, orthostatic hypotension, no ST changes, few ventricular ectopics) are traditionally used to judge readiness.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Intraoperative</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Arterial line before induction, central access, deep anaesthesia and analgesia; avoid triggers (histamine-releasing drugs, desflurane surges, metoclopramide, ephedrine, hypoventilation, surgical handling).</li><li><strong>Hypertension during tumour handling:</strong> short-acting vasodilators (<strong>phentolamine, nitroprusside, GTN, magnesium</strong>), esmolol for tachycardia, clevidipine or nicardipine.</li><li><strong>Hypotension after tumour vein ligation:</strong> fall in catecholamines with downregulated receptors and vasodilation → fluids, vasopressors (noradrenaline, vasopressin).</li><li><strong>Hypoglycaemia</strong> after removal (rebound insulin); postoperative monitoring in a high-dependency area; steroids if bilateral adrenalectomy.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"carcinoid-tumours\">Carcinoid tumours</h2>\n<ul><li>Neuroendocrine tumours (commonly small bowel) secreting <strong>serotonin</strong>, histamine, kinins and other mediators. <strong>Carcinoid syndrome</strong> (usually with liver metastases): flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm, and <strong>carcinoid heart disease</strong> (right-sided valve fibrosis: tricuspid regurgitation, pulmonary stenosis).</li><li><strong>Carcinoid crisis</strong> during anaesthesia: severe hypotension (or hypertension), bronchospasm, flushing — triggered by handling, catecholamines, histamine release.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> <strong>octreotide</strong> (somatostatin analogue) before and during surgery, and IV boluses for crisis; avoid histamine-releasing drugs and catecholamines where possible (they may worsen mediator release); vasopressin or phenylephrine for hypotension; echocardiography before surgery.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> order of α and β blockade; drugs for intraoperative hypertension; octreotide; carcinoid heart disease.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> discuss the preoperative preparation of a patient with phaeochromocytoma with the endocrinologist and surgeon.</li></ul></div>","neuropathic-cancer-pain":"<h2 id=\"neuropathic-pain\">Neuropathic pain</h2>\n<ul><li>Burning, shooting, electric-shock pain, allodynia, numbness; e.g. diabetic neuropathy, post-herpetic neuralgia, trigeminal neuralgia, radiculopathy, post-surgical nerve injury, phantom limb pain.</li><li><strong>First-line drugs</strong> (NICE): <strong>amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin or pregabalin</strong>, choosing by comorbidity and side effects, and switching if ineffective. <strong>Carbamazepine</strong> for trigeminal neuralgia. <strong>Topical capsaicin</strong> or lidocaine plasters for localised pain. Tramadol only for acute rescue.</li><li><strong>Mechanisms of drug action:</strong> tricyclics and SNRIs enhance descending inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) and block sodium channels; gabapentinoids bind the α<sub>2</sub>δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels (reducing transmitter release); antiarrhythmics (lidocaine, mexiletine) block sodium channels; opioids have limited efficacy and significant risks.</li><li>Gabapentinoids are controlled drugs in the UK (misuse potential; respiratory depression with opioids).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"complex-regional-pain-syndrome-crps\">Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS)</h2>\n<p>Pain disproportionate to the injury, with sensory, vasomotor (temperature and colour), sudomotor (sweating, oedema) and motor/trophic changes (<strong>Budapest criteria</strong>). Type I without and type II with a nerve injury. Early physiotherapy and desensitisation, analgesia, psychological support; specialist interventions (e.g. sympathetic blocks) in selected patients.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cancer-pain\">Cancer pain</h2>\n<ul><li>Often mixed nociceptive and neuropathic; also bone pain, visceral pain and incident pain.</li><li><strong>WHO approach:</strong> regular analgesia by the clock and by mouth, escalating as needed with adjuvants — now applied flexibly (strong opioids earlier when needed).</li><li><strong>Opioids:</strong> morphine as first-line strong opioid; alternatives in renal impairment (e.g. oxycodone with caution, fentanyl, alfentanil); <strong>breakthrough</strong> doses; laxatives and antiemetics; opioid rotation for toxicity.</li><li><strong>Adjuvants:</strong> steroids (nerve compression, liver capsule pain, raised ICP), NSAIDs (bone pain), radiotherapy (bone metastases), bisphosphonates, antineuropathic agents.</li><li><strong>Interventions:</strong> coeliac plexus neurolysis (pancreatic cancer), intrathecal drug delivery (implanted pumps), cordotomy (in specialist centres), vertebroplasty.</li><li><strong>Palliative care</strong> integration; end-of-life symptom control with syringe drivers.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"interventional-pain-techniques\">Interventional pain techniques</h2>\n<p>Epidural steroid injections (radicular pain), facet joint and medial branch blocks and radiofrequency denervation, sympathetic blocks (stellate, lumbar sympathetic, coeliac), spinal cord stimulation (neuropathic and refractory pain), peripheral nerve stimulation, <strong>TENS</strong>, and acupuncture — all within a multidisciplinary, evidence-based framework.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> first-line neuropathic drugs and mechanisms; CRPS criteria; coeliac plexus block indications.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> reviewing a patient with pancreatic cancer and uncontrolled pain on high-dose morphine.</li></ul></div>","neurosurgical-procedures":"<h2 id=\"posterior-fossa-surgery-and-the-sitting-position\">Posterior fossa surgery and the sitting position</h2>\n<ul><li>Brainstem proximity: cardiovascular changes (bradycardia, hypertension) from surgical stimulation; cranial nerve damage (airway protection postoperatively).</li><li><strong>Sitting or park bench</strong> positions improve access but risk <strong>venous air embolism (VAE)</strong>, pneumocephalus, hypotension and quadriplegia (neck flexion).</li><li><strong>VAE:</strong> detected by precordial Doppler or TOE (most sensitive), a sudden fall in ETCO<sub>2</sub>, rise in end-tidal nitrogen, hypotension, arrhythmias. Management: tell the surgeon to flood the field, lower the head, jugular compression, 100% O<sub>2</sub>, aspirate via a multi-orifice CVC in the right atrium, support the circulation. A patent foramen ovale risks paradoxical embolism.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"awake-craniotomy\">Awake craniotomy</h2>\n<p>For tumours near eloquent areas (speech, motor) and epilepsy surgery: scalp blocks (supraorbital, supratrochlear, zygomaticotemporal, auriculotemporal, greater and lesser occipital nerves) and pin-site infiltration, then either <strong>asleep–awake–asleep</strong> (with an SGA) or <strong>conscious sedation</strong> (propofol and remifentanil or dexmedetomidine). Patient selection and preparation are key; manage seizures (cold saline to the cortex), airway obstruction and agitation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pituitary-surgery-trans-sphenoidal\">Pituitary surgery (trans-sphenoidal)</h2>\n<p>Endocrine issues (acromegaly — difficult airway; Cushing's; hypopituitarism — steroid cover), nasal packs and bleeding, a smooth emergence, and postoperative <strong>diabetes insipidus</strong> (or SIADH).</p>\n<h2 id=\"spinal-surgery\">Spinal surgery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Scoliosis surgery:</strong> restrictive lung disease, associated neuromuscular disorders, major blood loss (tranexamic acid, cell salvage), prone positioning (eyes: <strong>postoperative visual loss</strong>; pressure areas; abdomen free), and <strong>spinal cord monitoring</strong> (SSEPs and MEPs with TIVA, no NMB; a wake-up test is a historical alternative).</li><li><strong>Spinal trauma</strong> (see spinal injury) and <strong>postoperative analgesia</strong> (intrathecal opioids, epidural catheters placed by the surgeon, multimodal).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"neuroradiology\">Neuroradiology</h2>\n<p><strong>Mechanical thrombectomy for stroke:</strong> time-critical; general anaesthesia or conscious sedation (local practice varies); avoid hypotension (keep systolic ≥ 140 mmHg before recanalisation, typical); transfer and radiology suite safety. <strong>Coiling</strong> of aneurysms (see SAH).</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-procedures\">Other procedures</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Shunt surgery</strong> (hydrocephalus; raised ICP at induction).</li><li><strong>Trigeminal neuralgia</strong> procedures (balloon compression or thermocoagulation of the Gasserian ganglion): brief deep anaesthesia, trigeminocardiac reflex (bradycardia).</li><li><strong>Stereotactic</strong> surgery (frame on the head: airway access).</li><li><strong>CSF drainage in aortic surgery</strong> protects the spinal cord (see vascular).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"prion-diseases\">Prion diseases</h2>\n<p>Neurosurgical instruments used in patients with, or at risk of, CJD must be handled according to specific guidance (quarantine or disposal).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> VAE detection and treatment; scalp nerves; evoked potential monitoring and anaesthetic effects.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> during a sitting-position craniotomy the ETCO<sub>2</sub> falls suddenly and BP drops: lead the response.</li></ul></div>","obstetric-analgesia-complications":"<h2 id=\"epidural-analgesia\">Epidural analgesia</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Low-dose regimens</strong> (e.g. 0.1% bupivacaine with fentanyl 2 µg/mL) preserve mobility and do not increase caesarean rates. <strong>Programmed intermittent epidural boluses</strong> with PCEA improve spread and reduce breakthrough pain compared with continuous infusions. <strong>CSE</strong> gives faster onset; <strong>dural puncture epidural</strong> (DPE) may improve quality.</li><li><strong>The failing epidural:</strong> assess the block (level, sidedness), the cause of pain (a full bladder, malposition, uterine rupture), pull back the catheter 1–2 cm, give a larger bolus of a stronger solution, and <strong>resite early</strong> if still inadequate — especially if the epidural may be needed for caesarean section.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"accidental-dural-puncture-and-pdph\">Accidental dural puncture and PDPH</h2>\n<ul><li>ADP with a 16–18G Tuohy needle causes PDPH in about half of women.</li><li>Options at the time: resite the epidural at another space, or thread an <strong>intrathecal catheter</strong> (label clearly; dose as a spinal).</li><li><strong>PDPH:</strong> a positional fronto-occipital headache within days, with neck stiffness, tinnitus, photophobia, and sometimes cranial nerve palsies. Differential: pre-eclampsia, migraine, <strong>cerebral venous sinus thrombosis</strong>, <strong>subdural haematoma</strong> (a complication of untreated PDPH), meningitis.</li><li><strong>Epidural blood patch</strong> (about 20 mL of autologous blood) is the most effective treatment; follow-up is essential. <strong>Sphenopalatine ganglion block</strong> and occipital nerve blocks have been used, with limited evidence.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"neurological-complications\">Neurological complications</h2>\n<ul><li>Most postpartum nerve injuries are <strong>obstetric</strong> (e.g. lumbosacral trunk compression, lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh — meralgia paraesthetica, common peroneal compression), not anaesthetic.</li><li><strong>Red flags:</strong> a block that is denser or longer than expected, new motor weakness after the block should have worn off, back pain, fever → urgent assessment and <strong>MRI</strong> to exclude epidural haematoma or abscess. Follow the 2020 neurological monitoring guideline (regular checks of motor function; escalation criteria).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"anticoagulation-and-thrombocytopenia\">Anticoagulation and thrombocytopenia</h2>\n<p>Plan neuraxial timing around LMWH doses; platelet thresholds as per local guidance; in HELLP or AFE check coagulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"non-obstetric-surgery-in-pregnancy\">Non-obstetric surgery in pregnancy</h2>\n<ul><li>Only urgent surgery; semi-elective in the <strong>second trimester</strong> if possible.</li><li>Maintain uteroplacental perfusion: avoid hypotension, hypoxia, hyper- or hypocapnia; left uterine displacement after about 20 weeks; aspiration prophylaxis and RSI; fetal monitoring according to gestation and obstetric advice; tocolysis if needed.</li><li>Most anaesthetic drugs in clinical doses are not known to be teratogenic; avoid unnecessary drugs in the first trimester. Radiation from necessary imaging is justified (shield where possible).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> PIEB; ADP management; PDPH differentials; timing of non-obstetric surgery.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> review a woman on day 2 with a headache after an epidural, and explain the options.</li></ul></div>","obstetric-emergencies":"<h2 id=\"amniotic-fluid-embolism-afe\">Amniotic fluid embolism (AFE)</h2>\n<ul><li>Sudden <strong>cardiorespiratory collapse</strong>, hypoxia, and <strong>coagulopathy (DIC)</strong> during labour, caesarean section or shortly after delivery; may include seizures and fetal distress. An anaphylactoid-type reaction to fetal material entering the maternal circulation.</li><li>A diagnosis of exclusion; management is supportive: <strong>ABC, CPR with left uterine displacement, resuscitative hysterotomy</strong> within about 5 minutes of arrest, massive transfusion with early <strong>fibrinogen replacement</strong>, and critical care (ECMO in some centres). Report to UKOSS.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"maternal-sepsis\">Maternal sepsis</h2>\n<p>A leading cause of maternal death. Recognise early (modified obstetric early warning scores; pregnancy masks signs), sepsis six, early antibiotics, source control (retained products, endometritis, chorioamnionitis, urinary, respiratory including influenza and COVID). Consider neuraxial block carefully (systemic infection, coagulopathy).</p>\n<h2 id=\"uterine-rupture-and-inversion\">Uterine rupture and inversion</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Rupture:</strong> usually through a previous caesarean scar; fetal distress, pain, bleeding, loss of station → immediate laparotomy (GA often needed).</li><li><strong>Inversion:</strong> shock out of proportion to blood loss (vagal); immediate manual replacement, uterine relaxation (GTN, volatile anaesthesia, terbutaline), then uterotonics.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-situations\">Other situations</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Multiple pregnancy:</strong> higher risk of pre-eclampsia, haemorrhage, and operative delivery; an epidural in labour is recommended to facilitate delivery of the second twin.</li><li><strong>Intrauterine death:</strong> compassionate care; coagulopathy risk if the fetus is retained for weeks; induction of labour with good analgesia.</li><li><strong>Premature delivery:</strong> steroids, magnesium for fetal neuroprotection (&lt; 30–32 weeks), neonatal team.</li><li><strong>VTE:</strong> thromboprophylaxis by risk assessment; timing of neuraxial blocks around LMWH.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"mbrrace-uk\">MBRRACE-UK</h2>\n<p>The confidential enquiries into maternal deaths report leading causes (thromboembolism, cardiac disease, psychiatric causes, epilepsy and stroke, sepsis, haemorrhage), highlight <strong>inequalities</strong> (higher mortality in Black and Asian women and those living in deprived areas), and make recommendations for anaesthetic practice (early escalation, multidisciplinary care, recognising the sick woman).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> features of AFE; uterine inversion management; MBRRACE themes.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> sudden collapse after delivery: lead resuscitation, then explain to the partner.</li></ul></div>","obstetric-ga-haemorrhage":"<h2 id=\"general-anaesthesia-for-caesarean-section\">General anaesthesia for caesarean section</h2>\n<ul><li>Indications: contraindication to or failure of neuraxial block, or when the time to establish neuraxial anaesthesia would harm the woman or baby (category 1).</li><li><strong>Preparation:</strong> WHO checklist, antacid prophylaxis (sodium citrate, H<sub>2</sub> antagonist), <strong>head-up (ramped) position</strong> with left uterine displacement, pre-oxygenation to ETO<sub>2</sub> ≥ 0.9 (HFNO can help), a smaller tube (e.g. 6.0–7.0 mm), video laryngoscope, bougie and second-generation SGA ready.</li><li><strong>RSI:</strong> propofol or thiopental and rocuronium or suxamethonium; opioid at induction is now common (e.g. to reduce the pressor response, particularly in pre-eclampsia), with the neonatal team informed.</li><li><strong>OAA/DAS failed intubation guideline:</strong> after failed intubation, rescue oxygenation with an SGA and a <strong>pre-planned decision to wake or proceed</strong>, based on the urgency and maternal/fetal condition.</li><li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> volatile (about 1 MAC; N<sub>2</sub>O optional) — risk of <strong>awareness</strong> is higher than in other surgery; reduce volatile after delivery if the uterus is atonic; oxytocin; multimodal analgesia with TAP or QL blocks; extubate awake in the head-up or lateral position.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"abnormal-placentation\">Abnormal placentation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Placenta praevia</strong> and <strong>placenta accreta spectrum</strong> (accreta, increta, percreta), especially with previous caesarean sections. Risk of massive haemorrhage.</li><li><strong>Planning:</strong> antenatal diagnosis (ultrasound, MRI), a multidisciplinary team at a specialist centre, elective delivery, cell salvage, blood availability, interventional radiology (balloons), large-bore access and arterial line, and discussion of anaesthetic technique (neuraxial with conversion to GA, or GA from the start).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"major-obstetric-haemorrhage\">Major obstetric haemorrhage</h2>\n<ul><li>Defined variably (e.g. &gt; 1,000 mL with signs of shock, or &gt; 1,500 mL); <strong>underestimation is common</strong> — measure blood loss (weigh swabs).</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> call for help and activate the major haemorrhage protocol; ABC with large-bore access; uterotonics (oxytocin, ergometrine, carboprost, misoprostol); <strong>tranexamic acid 1 g</strong> early; blood and products; <strong>fibrinogen replacement guided by Clauss fibrinogen or viscoelastic tests (FIBTEM)</strong>, aiming for about ≥ 2 g/L; maintain platelets &gt; 75 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L; correct calcium, temperature and acidosis; <strong>cell salvage</strong>; surgical measures (bimanual compression, balloon tamponade, B-Lynch suture, uterine artery ligation, hysterectomy) and interventional radiology.</li><li>Pregnancy-related coagulopathy is often <strong>fibrinogen depletion</strong> (especially in placental abruption and amniotic fluid embolism).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"refusal-of-blood\">Refusal of blood</h2>\n<p>Jehovah's Witnesses may refuse whole blood and major components but individuals vary in what they accept (cell salvage, fractions such as fibrinogen concentrate or albumin). Discuss in detail antenatally, document an advance decision, optimise haemoglobin, and plan early escalation (cell salvage, tranexamic acid, early surgical control). A competent adult's refusal must be respected.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> aspiration prophylaxis; OAA/DAS principles; fibrinogen targets; placenta accreta risks.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> lead a major obstetric haemorrhage simulation, communicating with the obstetrician, lab and haematologist.</li></ul></div>","obstructed-airway-infections":"<h2 id=\"principles\">Principles</h2>\n<p>Keep the patient calm and sitting up; high-flow oxygen; <strong>senior anaesthetist and ENT/maxillofacial surgeon early</strong>; imaging only if the patient is stable; temporising measures (nebulised adrenaline, steroids, heliox); a <strong>clear plan with a double set-up</strong> for front-of-neck access (or awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia). Avoid sedatives on the ward.</p>\n<h2 id=\"infections\">Infections</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Adult supraglottitis/epiglottitis:</strong> sore throat, odynophagia, muffled voice, drooling; nasendoscopy (by experienced hands) shows swelling. Many settle with antibiotics, steroids and close observation in a monitored area; deteriorating patients need a secure airway (ATI or awake FONA).</li><li><strong>Ludwig's angina</strong> (bilateral submandibular cellulitis) and <strong>dental abscesses:</strong> trismus (may not improve with anaesthesia if caused by swelling rather than pain), floor-of-mouth elevation, tongue displacement. ATI (nasal) or awake tracheostomy are common strategies.</li><li><strong>Retropharyngeal and peritonsillar (quinsy) abscess:</strong> risk of rupture and aspiration with instrumentation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"maxillofacial-trauma\">Maxillofacial trauma</h2>\n<p>Airway at risk from bleeding, swelling, loss of tongue support (bilateral mandibular fractures), and base-of-skull fractures (avoid nasal instrumentation). Cervical spine protection; may need submental intubation or tracheostomy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"anterior-mediastinal-mass\">Anterior mediastinal mass</h2>\n<ul><li>Lymphoma, thymoma, germ cell tumours, retrosternal goitre. <strong>Symptoms</strong> (orthopnoea, stridor, SVC obstruction, syncope) and <strong>CT</strong> (tracheal compression &gt; 50%, bronchial compression) predict risk.</li><li><strong>General anaesthesia can cause catastrophic airway collapse or cardiovascular collapse</strong> (loss of muscle tone, reduced FRC, compression of the airway or great vessels).</li><li>Strategies: obtain tissue diagnosis under local anaesthesia if possible; pre-treatment (steroids, radiotherapy) where appropriate; if GA is needed, maintain <strong>spontaneous ventilation</strong>, avoid neuromuscular blockade until the airway is secured, have a rigid bronchoscope and surgeon ready, change position (lateral or prone) if collapse occurs, and consider <strong>ECMO standby or pre-emptive femoral cannulation</strong> in high-risk cases.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> features predicting risk with mediastinal masses; trismus causes; temporising measures.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> plan with the surgeon for a patient with Ludwig's angina who needs drainage, including consent and backup plans.</li></ul></div>","ophthalmic-anaesthesia":"<h2 id=\"patients\">Patients</h2>\n<p>Often <strong>elderly with comorbidities</strong> (diabetes, hypertension, cognitive impairment, anticoagulants); most surgery (e.g. cataract) is done under <strong>local anaesthesia</strong> as day cases. Anticoagulants are usually continued for sub-Tenon's and topical techniques.</p>\n<h2 id=\"local-anaesthetic-techniques\">Local anaesthetic techniques</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Features</th><th>Risks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Topical</strong> (drops, gel) ± intracameral</td><td>Cataract surgery; no akinesia</td><td>Discomfort, patient movement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sub-Tenon's</strong></td><td>Blunt cannula into the sub-Tenon's space (inferonasal); akinesia with larger volumes</td><td>Chemosis, subconjunctival haemorrhage; serious complications rare</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Peribulbar (extraconal)</strong></td><td>Needle outside the muscle cone</td><td>Globe perforation (especially axial length &gt; 26 mm), haemorrhage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Retrobulbar (intraconal)</strong></td><td>Needle inside the cone; dense akinesia</td><td>Retrobulbar haemorrhage, optic nerve injury, <strong>brainstem anaesthesia</strong> (spread along the optic nerve sheath → apnoea, loss of consciousness), globe perforation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"intraocular-pressure-iop\">Intraocular pressure (IOP)</h2>\n<ul><li>Normal 10–20 mmHg; determined by aqueous humour production (ciliary body) and drainage (trabecular meshwork, canal of Schlemm), choroidal blood volume and extraocular muscle tone.</li><li><strong>Increased by:</strong> coughing, straining, vomiting, hypercapnia, hypoxia, venous congestion (head-down, tight ties), external pressure (facemask), <strong>suxamethonium</strong> (transient), ketamine (slight), laryngoscopy.</li><li><strong>Decreased by:</strong> most IV and volatile anaesthetics, hyperventilation, head-up tilt, <strong>acetazolamide</strong>, <strong>mannitol</strong>, timolol.</li><li><strong>Oculocardiac reflex:</strong> trigeminal afferents, vagal efferents (see anatomy).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"specific-situations\">Specific situations</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Penetrating eye injury with a full stomach:</strong> balance aspiration risk against extrusion of ocular contents from raised IOP. RSI with <strong>rocuronium</strong> (avoid suxamethonium if possible, although its IOP rise is small and it has been used safely), avoid coughing and pressure on the eye; deep extubation is not appropriate in the full stomach — smooth awake extubation.</li><li><strong>Intraocular gas</strong> (SF<sub>6</sub>, C<sub>3</sub>F<sub>8</sub> after vitreoretinal surgery): <strong>avoid nitrous oxide</strong> for weeks (expansion, raised IOP, blindness); avoid air travel.</li><li><strong>Strabismus surgery</strong> in children: oculocardiac reflex and high PONV risk.</li><li><strong>Operating conditions:</strong> a still patient, a soft eye, a clear field, and a lying-flat, comfortable patient (claustrophobia, orthopnoea, cough or tremor can make local anaesthesia unsuitable).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> block anatomy and complications; factors affecting IOP; intraocular gas and N<sub>2</sub>O.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient with a penetrating eye injury who has just eaten: discuss the plan with the ophthalmologist.</li></ul></div>","paeds-common-surgery":"<h2 id=\"day-surgery-in-children\">Day surgery in children</h2>\n<p>Most paediatric surgery is day case: fitness, a suitable home, and parents who understand the instructions. Exclusions include some ex-premature infants, significant comorbidity, OSA needing overnight monitoring (e.g. adenotonsillectomy in severe OSA or young age), and difficult social circumstances.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tonsillectomy\">Tonsillectomy</h2>\n<p>OSA versus recurrent infection (OSA children are more sensitive to opioids and at risk of postoperative respiratory events); shared airway; analgesia (paracetamol, ibuprofen, dexamethasone, careful morphine — <strong>no codeine in under-18s</strong>); PONV prophylaxis; bleeding (see the bleeding tonsil).</p>\n<h2 id=\"fluids\">Fluids</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Deficit:</strong> assess dehydration clinically (mild &lt; 5%, moderate 5–10%, severe &gt; 10%); replace over 24–48 h (faster for shock).</li><li><strong>Resuscitation:</strong> isotonic crystalloid <strong>10 mL/kg</strong> boluses with reassessment (NICE NG29; 20 mL/kg is used in some settings).</li><li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> isotonic fluids (e.g. 0.9% saline with 5% glucose, or balanced solutions) using the 4-2-1 rule, often <strong>restricted to about two-thirds</strong> postoperatively because of ADH release; check electrolytes daily. <strong>Avoid hypotonic fluids</strong> (hyponatraemic encephalopathy).</li><li><strong>Intraoperative:</strong> replace losses with balanced crystalloid; glucose for infants and at-risk children.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"blood\">Blood</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Estimated blood volume:</strong> preterm ~90–100 mL/kg, term neonate ~85–90 mL/kg, infant ~80 mL/kg, child ~75 mL/kg.</li><li><strong>Allowable blood loss</strong> ≈ EBV × (starting Hct − target Hct) / starting Hct (some versions use the average Hct).</li><li>Transfusion volume (mL) ≈ weight (kg) × desired rise in Hb (g/L) × about 0.4–0.5 (e.g. 4–5 mL/kg per 10 g/L; follow local formulae). Products in mL/kg (FFP 10–15 mL/kg, platelets 10–15 mL/kg, cryoprecipitate 5–10 mL/kg).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"tiva-in-children\">TIVA in children</h2>\n<p>Paediatric models (e.g. Kataria, Paedfusor; Eleveld covers a wide age range) for propofol; larger doses per kg than adults; injection pain; watch for propofol infusion syndrome with long, high-dose infusions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"regional-anaesthesia-in-children\">Regional anaesthesia in children</h2>\n<p>Usually performed under general anaesthesia (accepted practice). <strong>Caudal</strong> (e.g. levobupivacaine 0.2%, 0.5–1 mL/kg depending on the height needed, keeping within the maximum dose; additives such as clonidine prolong it), penile block, ilioinguinal/iliohypogastric, rectus sheath, TAP, and peripheral limb blocks under ultrasound; epidurals and spinals in specialist centres. Doses by weight within limits; test dose with adrenaline (T-wave changes).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> EBV by age; allowable blood loss formula; fluid choices; caudal dosing.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> managing a 3-year-old for day-case circumcision including analgesia, and discussing discharge with the parent.</li></ul></div>","paeds-complex":"<h2 id=\"congenital-anomalies\">Congenital anomalies</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tracheo-oesophageal fistula / oesophageal atresia:</strong> frothy secretions, inability to pass a nasogastric tube; associated <strong>VACTERL</strong> anomalies (check the heart); positioning the tube below the fistula but above the carina (or blocking the fistula), avoiding gastric distension with IPPV.</li><li><strong>Congenital diaphragmatic hernia:</strong> pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension; gentle ventilation (permissive hypercapnia), avoid mask ventilation (gut distension); surgery once stabilised.</li><li><strong>Congenital heart disease:</strong> understand the lesion and the current physiology (shunt direction, pulmonary blood flow, duct dependency, Fontan circulation — passive pulmonary flow sensitive to PVR and intrathoracic pressure); avoid air bubbles (paradoxical embolism with right-to-left shunts); involve cardiac anaesthetists; endocarditis prophylaxis only in specific high-risk situations.</li><li><strong>Trisomy 21:</strong> airway (large tongue, subglottic stenosis — smaller tube), <strong>atlantoaxial instability</strong> (neutral neck), congenital heart disease (AVSD), OSA, hypothyroidism, bradycardia with sevoflurane induction, learning disability.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"neurodiversity-and-learning-disability\">Neurodiversity and learning disability</h2>\n<p>Autism and learning disability: reasonable adjustments (first on the list, quiet environment, familiar carers, hospital passport, flexible induction, premedication, possibly restraint only as a last resort under legal frameworks), capacity assessment (from 16) and best interests decisions, combining procedures under one anaesthetic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-sick-and-injured-child\">The sick and injured child</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Recognition:</strong> ABCDE with age-specific normal values; causes of deterioration differ from adults: arrest is usually <strong>hypoxic</strong>, preceded by respiratory failure or shock.</li><li><strong>Specific conditions:</strong> meningococcal sepsis (fluid, antibiotics, inotropes, early intubation), bronchiolitis, severe asthma, status epilepticus, DKA (cautious fluids — cerebral oedema risk), trauma (head injury is the main cause of death; non-accidental injury must be considered).</li><li><strong>Airway:</strong> cuffed tubes are now standard in critical illness (check cuff pressure); RSI with ketamine or reduced propofol doses; atropine for bradycardia.</li><li><strong>Resuscitation (RCUK PLS):</strong> 5 rescue breaths, 15:2 compressions, adrenaline 10 µg/kg, amiodarone 5 mg/kg for shockable rhythms, shocks 4 J/kg.</li><li><strong>Transfer:</strong> usually by regional paediatric retrieval teams; the local team stabilises and prepares.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Down syndrome issues; TOF positioning; Fontan physiology; paediatric resuscitation doses.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> stabilising a 2-year-old with meningococcal sepsis before the retrieval team arrives.</li></ul></div>","paeds-consent-safeguarding":"<h2 id=\"consent\">Consent</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Parental responsibility:</strong> mothers automatically; fathers if married to the mother at birth or named on the birth certificate (in England and Wales after 2003); others by court order or adoption. One person with PR can usually consent.</li><li><strong>Gillick competence:</strong> a child under 16 who has sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed can consent. (The <strong>Fraser guidelines</strong> specifically concern contraceptive advice.)</li><li><strong>16–17-year-olds</strong> are presumed competent to consent (Family Law Reform Act 1969) and the Mental Capacity Act applies from 16. Refusal of life-saving treatment by a competent young person may be overridden by the court (or, in limited circumstances, those with PR) — seek senior and legal advice.</li><li><strong>Emergencies:</strong> treat in the child's best interests when consent cannot be obtained in time.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"restraint-and-holding\">Restraint and holding</h2>\n<ul><li>Clinical holding may be needed for essential procedures in young children. Use the least restrictive approach for the shortest time, with explanation, parental involvement where possible, and documentation. Consider alternatives (premedication, distraction, postponement). For older children and those with learning disabilities, restraint must be justified in their best interests and proportionate (MCA).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"research\">Research</h2>\n<p>Children can participate in research with consent from those with PR and the child's assent where possible; ethics approval considers the balance of risk and benefit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"safeguarding\">Safeguarding</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Recognise:</strong> injuries inconsistent with the history or developmental stage, delayed presentation, bruising in non-mobile infants, patterned injuries, torn frenulum, unexplained fractures, repeated attendances, concerning interactions.</li><li><strong>Act:</strong> ensure the child's immediate safety, record findings objectively (body maps, photographs by trained staff), share concerns with the named safeguarding lead and follow local procedures; do not investigate yourself or confront parents. Information sharing to protect a child is justified even without consent.</li><li>Anaesthetists may see concerns during examination under anaesthesia, dental GA, or trauma care.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> PR rules; Gillick; 16–17-year-olds; safeguarding steps.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a 15-year-old refuses an anaesthetic for a necessary operation — explore capacity and next steps with the family.</li></ul></div>","periarrest-resuscitation":"<h2 id=\"the-approach-rcuk-peri-arrest-algorithms\">The approach (RCUK peri-arrest algorithms)</h2>\n<p>Assess with ABCDE, give oxygen, obtain IV access and a 12-lead ECG, correct electrolytes, and look for <strong>adverse features</strong>: <strong>shock, syncope, myocardial ischaemia, severe heart failure</strong>. Their presence determines the urgency and route of treatment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tachycardias\">Tachycardias</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rhythm</th><th>Stable</th><th>With adverse features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Broad regular</strong> (VT unless proven otherwise)</td><td>Amiodarone (300 mg IV over 10–60 min)</td><td><strong>Synchronised DC cardioversion</strong> (up to three attempts, with sedation or anaesthesia), then amiodarone</td></tr><tr><td>Broad irregular</td><td>AF with BBB (treat as AF), pre-excited AF (avoid AV nodal blockers; cardiovert), polymorphic VT/<strong>torsades</strong> (magnesium 2 g)</td><td>Cardioversion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Narrow regular</strong> (SVT, AVNRT)</td><td><strong>Vagal manoeuvres</strong> (modified Valsalva), then <strong>adenosine</strong> (6, 12, 18 mg rapid boluses; flutter becomes visible); β-blocker or verapamil</td><td>Cardioversion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Narrow irregular</strong> (AF)</td><td>Rate control (β-blocker, diltiazem, digoxin in heart failure); anticoagulation assessment; rhythm control if onset &lt; 48 h or anticoagulated</td><td>Cardioversion</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Synchronised cardioversion:</strong> the shock is timed to the R wave (to avoid inducing VF); higher energies for broad-complex rhythms and AF; conscious patients need sedation or anaesthesia (usually by an anaesthetist, considering fasting and airway).</p>\n<h2 id=\"bradycardias\">Bradycardias</h2>\n<ul><li>Treat if adverse features or <strong>risk of asystole</strong> (recent asystole, Mobitz II, complete heart block with broad QRS, ventricular pauses &gt; 3 s).</li><li><strong>Atropine 500 µg IV</strong> (repeat to 3 mg); then <strong>isoprenaline</strong> or <strong>adrenaline</strong> infusion (or other drugs: aminophylline, glucagon for β-blocker overdose, dopamine); <strong>transcutaneous pacing</strong>, then <strong>transvenous pacing</strong> by an expert. <strong>Percussion pacing</strong> (fist pacing) is a temporising option in some circumstances.</li><li>Heart transplant recipients do not respond to atropine.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"thrombolysis\">Thrombolysis</h2>\n<p>For <strong>massive PE</strong> with cardiac arrest or shock (alteplase), and in some STEMI and stroke settings where primary PCI or thrombectomy is not available. Continue CPR for 60–90 minutes after thrombolysis in arrest. Contraindications (recent surgery, bleeding) are weighed against the risk of death.</p>\n<h2 id=\"anaphylaxis-stage-2\">Anaphylaxis (Stage 2)</h2>\n<p>Leadership of the crisis, refractory anaphylaxis (adrenaline infusion, vasopressin, glucagon, ECMO), investigation and allergy referral, and patient communication (see the Primary notes). Recognise allergy versus non-allergic reactions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cardiac-arrest-in-special-positions-and-settings\">Cardiac arrest in special positions and settings</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Prone position</strong> (e.g. spinal surgery): start CPR prone with compressions between the scapulae (a hand or sandbag under the sternum as counter-pressure) and defibrillation with pads placed posterolaterally, while preparing to turn the patient supine; turn when it can be done quickly and safely.</li><li><strong>Open-chest cardiac compression</strong> and <strong>resuscitative thoracotomy</strong> (penetrating trauma, post-cardiac surgery) — specialist indications.</li><li><strong>Post-arrest care:</strong> see temperature control in ECMO and advanced support.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> algorithm decisions; adenosine doses and interactions; pacing options; prone CPR.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient in recovery with a broad-complex tachycardia at 180/min and a BP of 70/40: lead the team.</li></ul></div>","pre-eclampsia-hellp":"<h2 id=\"pathophysiology\">Pathophysiology</h2>\n<p>Abnormal placentation → placental ischaemia → release of anti-angiogenic factors (e.g. sFlt-1) → <strong>widespread endothelial dysfunction</strong>: vasoconstriction, capillary leak, activation of coagulation and platelets. Affects every organ: brain (eclampsia, stroke, PRES), kidneys (proteinuria, AKI), liver (HELLP, rupture), lungs (pulmonary oedema), blood (thrombocytopenia, DIC), placenta (fetal growth restriction, abruption).</p>\n<h2 id=\"diagnosis-and-severity-nice-ng133\">Diagnosis and severity (NICE NG133)</h2>\n<ul><li>New hypertension (≥ 140/90) after 20 weeks with proteinuria <strong>or</strong> other maternal organ dysfunction or uteroplacental dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Severe features:</strong> BP ≥ 160/110, headache, visual disturbance, epigastric or right upper quadrant pain, clonus, low platelets, rising creatinine or transaminases, pulmonary oedema, HELLP (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"management\">Management</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Antihypertensives:</strong> oral labetalol (first-line), nifedipine, methyldopa; IV labetalol or hydralazine for severe hypertension. Target around 135/85.</li><li><strong>Magnesium sulphate</strong> (loading dose then infusion) for severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia: prevents and treats seizures (better than phenytoin or diazepam). Monitor reflexes, respiratory rate, urine output and SpO<sub>2</sub>; reduce dose in oliguria; <strong>calcium gluconate</strong> for toxicity; potentiates neuromuscular block.</li><li><strong>Fluid restriction</strong> (e.g. about 80 mL/h total unless ongoing losses) to avoid pulmonary oedema.</li><li><strong>Timing of delivery</strong> is the definitive treatment, balancing maternal risk and fetal maturity (steroids for fetal lung maturity).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"anaesthesia\">Anaesthesia</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Neuraxial is preferred</strong> for labour and caesarean section (reduces stress response, avoids airway risks). Check the <strong>platelet count</strong> (trend matters; many units accept &gt; 75 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L without other coagulopathy) and coagulation if platelets are low or HELLP.</li><li>Hypotension after spinal is usually less marked than in normal pregnancy; use vasopressors cautiously.</li><li><strong>General anaesthesia:</strong> airway oedema (smaller tubes), and <strong>attenuate the hypertensive response to laryngoscopy</strong> (risk of intracranial haemorrhage) with, for example, remifentanil, alfentanil, labetalol or magnesium; beware magnesium–NMB interactions.</li><li>Postpartum: seizures and severe hypertension may occur in the first days; continue monitoring.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> severe features; magnesium effects and toxicity; platelet thresholds; drugs for the pressor response.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> assess a woman with severe pre-eclampsia who needs caesarean section and explain your anaesthetic choice to her.</li></ul></div>","professional-leadership":"<h2 id=\"professional-behaviours\">Professional behaviours</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Guiding less experienced colleagues:</strong> supervision (appropriate to their level), teaching, giving feedback, and knowing when to escalate. <strong>Role-modelling</strong> respectful, safe behaviour.</li><li><strong>Complex patients:</strong> formulate individualised plans with the patient (risks and benefits explained in understandable terms, shared decisions), and recognise your own limits.</li><li><strong>Behaviour and safety:</strong> incivility and unprofessional behaviour harm team performance and patient safety; speak up and support colleagues; reflect on your own behaviour.</li><li><strong>Difficult situations:</strong> leading under pressure, maintaining situation awareness, and professional judgement in emergencies.</li><li><strong>Wellbeing:</strong> the effects of shift work, fatigue, stress and lifestyle on health and performance; strategies (rest, rota design, peer support, professional help).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"management-and-organisation\">Management and organisation</h2>\n<ul><li>How anaesthetic services are organised: theatres, preoperative assessment, critical care, pain, obstetrics, <strong>day surgery</strong> (facilities, staffing, protocols, audit), and links with regional tertiary services and networks.</li><li>Departmental management structures (clinical directors, college tutors, rota coordinators), job planning, clinical governance, risk management, complaints and litigation.</li><li><strong>Legal and ethical frameworks:</strong> consent and shared decision-making, confidentiality, organ donation ethics, Mental Capacity Act, and Mental Health Act.</li><li><strong>Digital working:</strong> electronic records and prescribing, information governance, data protection, and safe use of digital tools and AI.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"safeguarding-adults-and-capacity\">Safeguarding adults and capacity</h2>\n<ul><li>Principles: <strong>empowerment, prevention, proportionality, protection, partnership, accountability</strong>.</li><li>Communicate with safeguarding teams, sharing information appropriately (confidentiality versus protection).</li><li><strong>Mental Capacity Act</strong> in practice: capacity assessment, best interests decisions for procedures such as <strong>ECT</strong> and dental treatment under GA, deprivation of liberty safeguards, and independent advocates.</li><li><strong>People with learning disabilities, autism, acute confusion, dementia and mental illness:</strong> physical and psychological needs, reasonable adjustments, involvement of carers and those who support the patient (who have important roles in the perioperative period).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> safeguarding principles; MCA in ECT; day surgery organisation.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a professional conversation with a junior colleague who made an error, or planning dental GA for an adult with severe learning disability who lacks capacity.</li></ul></div>","qi-safety-stage2":"<h2 id=\"improvement-science\">Improvement science</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Model for Improvement</strong> and <strong>PDSA cycles</strong>, driver diagrams (aim → primary and secondary drivers → change ideas), process mapping, and stakeholder engagement.</li><li><strong>Measures:</strong> outcome, process and <strong>balancing</strong> measures (checking for unintended consequences).</li><li><strong>Data for improvement</strong> (frequent, small samples, plotted over time) versus <strong>data for assurance</strong> (performance against standards, e.g. national audits) versus <strong>data for research</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"variation\">Variation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Common-cause variation</strong> is inherent in a stable process; <strong>special-cause variation</strong> signals a change (improvement or a problem).</li><li><strong>Run charts</strong> (rules: shifts, trends, runs, astronomical points) and <strong>statistical process control (SPC) charts</strong> with control limits distinguish them. Reacting to common-cause variation as if it were special (\"tampering\") makes things worse; unwarranted variation between units is a target for improvement.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"reliable-care\">Reliable care</h2>\n<p>Human factors design (standardisation, checklists, forcing functions, reminders, simplification, reducing reliance on memory), high-reliability organisation principles, and matching the <strong>expertise and resources to the risk</strong> (senior presence for high-risk cases, the right place for postoperative care, out-of-hours staffing).</p>\n<h2 id=\"following-up-critical-incidents\">Following up critical incidents</h2>\n<ul><li>Immediate care and support of the patient; debrief; <strong>report</strong>; investigation proportionate to harm (systems-focused learning reviews, e.g. under PSIRF).</li><li><strong>Duty of candour:</strong> tell the patient (and family), apologise, explain what is known, what will happen next, and provide written follow-up for notifiable incidents.</li><li><strong>Unexpected difficult intubation:</strong> explain to the patient, document fully (grade, what worked, what failed), give a <strong>difficult airway letter</strong> to the patient and GP, add a clinical alert, and encourage registration with a difficult airway database where available (DAS recommendations).</li><li>Staff support (second victims) and learning shared through M&amp;M meetings.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> types of measures; common versus special cause variation; run chart rules.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> describing a QI project to a panel, or explaining an unexpected difficult airway to the patient afterwards.</li></ul></div>","ra-safety":"<h2 id=\"before-the-block\">Before the block</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Consent:</strong> discuss benefits and specific risks (block failure, LAST, nerve injury — transient symptoms in a few per cent, permanent injury rare; block-specific risks such as phrenic palsy or pneumothorax). Document the discussion.</li><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> coagulation and anticoagulant timing (especially for deep or non-compressible blocks), infection, pre-existing neuropathy (document a baseline examination), and patient cooperation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"performing-the-block\">Performing the block</h2>\n<p><strong>Stop Before You Block</strong> (a moment immediately before needle insertion to confirm the site and side, often with the assistant; wrong-side blocks are \"never events\"), full monitoring, IV access, resuscitation equipment and lipid emulsion immediately available, asepsis, ultrasound with or without nerve stimulation, <strong>injection pressure</strong> monitoring, incremental injection and aspiration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nerve-injury\">Nerve injury</h2>\n<p>Mechanisms: needle trauma, intraneural injection (especially intrafascicular), toxicity of the local anaesthetic, ischaemia; plus surgical, positioning, tourniquet and patient factors. <strong>Management:</strong> examine and document, involve the surgeon, early neurology referral and nerve conduction studies for significant deficits, and follow-up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"regional-anaesthesia-in-trauma-patients\">Regional anaesthesia in trauma patients</h2>\n<p>Benefits (analgesia, reduced opioids, delirium prevention, earlier mobilisation) versus concerns (masking <strong>compartment syndrome</strong> — rare; use lower concentrations, avoid dense motor block, communicate and monitor; coagulopathy; consent in acutely injured or confused patients).</p>\n<h2 id=\"quality-and-governance\">Quality and governance</h2>\n<p>Standardised documentation, block rooms and dedicated teams, training (the RA-UK and RCoA curriculum resources), audit of success, complications and patient satisfaction, and national registries.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> causes of nerve injury; Stop Before You Block; anticoagulation rules.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient has persistent numbness three days after an interscalene block: assess and plan follow-up.</li></ul></div>","regional-lower-limb":"<h2 id=\"hip\">Hip</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Fascia iliaca</strong> (infra- or supra-inguinal): hip fracture analgesia from the emergency department onwards (recommended in UK guidance), and hip surgery.</li><li><strong>PENG (pericapsular nerve group) block:</strong> targets the articular branches of the femoral and accessory obturator nerves between the iliopubic eminence and psoas tendon; aims to spare quadriceps strength.</li><li><strong>Neuraxial</strong> (spinal) for surgery; lumbar plexus block in specialist hands (deep; epidural spread risk).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"knee\">Knee</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Adductor canal block</strong> (saphenous nerve and nerve to vastus medialis): analgesia after knee arthroplasty with <strong>less quadriceps weakness</strong> than femoral block (better mobilisation, fewer falls).</li><li><strong>iPACK</strong> (infiltration between the popliteal artery and the capsule of the knee): posterior knee pain.</li><li><strong>Femoral nerve block:</strong> effective but weakens quadriceps.</li><li>Periarticular local infiltration by the surgeon is also common.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"lower-leg-ankle-and-foot\">Lower leg, ankle and foot</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Popliteal sciatic block</strong> (before the bifurcation into tibial and common peroneal nerves) + <strong>saphenous</strong> block for the medial ankle: foot and ankle surgery.</li><li><strong>Ankle block:</strong> tibial, deep peroneal, superficial peroneal, sural and saphenous nerves: forefoot surgery.</li><li>Beware <strong>compartment syndrome</strong> in trauma (e.g. tibial fractures): discuss with the surgeon; lower concentrations, monitoring, and clear documentation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-neuraxial-versus-peripheral-blocks\">Choosing neuraxial versus peripheral blocks</h2>\n<p>Consider the procedure, anticoagulation, the patient's comorbidities (e.g. severe AS), the need for early mobilisation, urinary retention, and patient preference. Spinal anaesthesia with short-acting agents supports day-case lower limb surgery.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> nerves covered by each block; motor-sparing options; compartment syndrome concerns.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> plan analgesia for a total knee replacement patient who wants to mobilise the same day.</li></ul></div>","regional-upper-limb":"<h2 id=\"choosing-an-approach\">Choosing an approach</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Surgery</th><th>Block</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shoulder, clavicle (lateral), proximal humerus</td><td><strong>Interscalene</strong> (C5–6)</td><td>Near-certain hemidiaphragmatic paresis at usual volumes; alternatives: <strong>superior trunk block</strong>, suprascapular (+ axillary) nerve blocks, low-volume techniques</td></tr><tr><td>Humerus, elbow, forearm, hand</td><td><strong>Supraclavicular</strong> (\"the spinal of the arm\")</td><td>Dense and fast; pneumothorax risk low with ultrasound; phrenic block possible</td></tr><tr><td>Elbow, forearm, hand</td><td><strong>Infraclavicular</strong> (cords around the axillary artery, deep to pectoralis)</td><td>Good for catheters; deep and angled</td></tr><tr><td>Forearm and hand</td><td><strong>Axillary</strong></td><td>Separate musculocutaneous injection; safest in coagulopathy (compressible)</td></tr><tr><td>Hand (rescue)</td><td>Forearm/elbow blocks of median, ulnar, radial nerves</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pharmacology\">Pharmacology</h2>\n<ul><li>Choice of agent by duration: lidocaine or prilocaine (short), levobupivacaine or ropivacaine (long).</li><li><strong>Adjuvants:</strong> <strong>dexamethasone</strong> (perineural or IV, prolongs analgesia; IV is similarly effective and avoids perineural use off-label), clonidine, dexmedetomidine; adrenaline (marks intravascular injection; limits absorption). Opioids add little to peripheral blocks.</li><li><strong>Catheters</strong> extend analgesia (e.g. interscalene for major shoulder surgery).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"getting-a-good-ultrasound-image\">Getting a good ultrasound image</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Probe choice</strong> (high-frequency linear for most upper limb blocks), <strong>depth</strong> (target in the middle of the screen), <strong>gain</strong> (uniform brightness), <strong>focus</strong> at the target, <strong>PART</strong> manoeuvres (pressure, alignment, rotation, tilt) to improve nerve visibility (anisotropy), and colour Doppler to identify vessels.</li><li>Artefacts: acoustic enhancement deep to arteries (can mimic nerves), shadowing, reverberation from the needle.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"safety\">Safety</h2>\n<p>Sterile technique; monitoring; <strong>injection pressure monitoring</strong> (avoid &gt; 15 psi); hydrodissection; the needle tip seen at all times; incremental injection with aspiration; <strong>Stop Before You Block</strong>; checking coagulation for deep or non-compressible blocks; documentation; limb protection advice; follow-up of neurological symptoms.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> matching blocks to surgery; phrenic-sparing options; adjuvants.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> discussing block options for a patient with COPD having shoulder surgery.</li></ul></div>","remote-ect-mri":"<h2 id=\"principles\">Principles</h2>\n<p>The <strong>same standards</strong> of equipment, monitoring, assistance and recovery as theatre, with extra planning: an unfamiliar environment, limited space and access, darkness, lack of skilled help, distance from theatres, and patients who may be sick or uncooperative. Check the equipment, oxygen supply, suction, drugs, communication and escape routes before starting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mri\">MRI</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hazards:</strong> the static magnetic field (projectiles, displacement of implants, device malfunction), gradient fields (noise, peripheral nerve stimulation), radiofrequency energy (heating and <strong>burns</strong> from looped cables and ECG leads), quenching of helium (asphyxia).</li><li><strong>Zones</strong> with controlled access; screening of patients and staff; <strong>MR-safe or MR-conditional</strong> anaesthetic machines, monitors and pumps (or long lines through waveguides).</li><li>Monitoring is often degraded (ECG artefact); capnography with long sampling lines (delay).</li><li>Children and patients who cannot lie still often need GA or deep sedation; plan recovery nearby.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"electroconvulsive-therapy-ect\">Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)</h2>\n<ul><li>Treatment for severe depression and other psychiatric conditions; a generalised seizure is induced under GA with muscle relaxation.</li><li><strong>Physiology:</strong> initial <strong>parasympathetic</strong> response (bradycardia, even asystole) followed by a <strong>sympathetic</strong> surge (tachycardia, hypertension) — risky in cardiovascular disease; raised ICP and IOP; myalgia.</li><li><strong>Anaesthesia:</strong> a short GA (propofol, methohexital, etomidate or ketamine — propofol shortens seizure duration; etomidate and ketamine lengthen it), <strong>suxamethonium</strong> (modified seizure; a tourniquet on one arm can show the seizure), hyperventilation (lowers seizure threshold), bite block, and treatments repeated often (twice weekly courses).</li><li><strong>Consent and capacity:</strong> ECT may be given to patients lacking capacity under the Mental Health Act and Mental Capacity Act with specific safeguards; respect valid advance refusals.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-remote-sites\">Other remote sites</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Endoscopy</strong> (ERCP in the prone position, shared airway; propofol sedation by anaesthetists for complex cases).</li><li><strong>Cardiology</strong> (cardioversion — brief anaesthesia with a full stomach risk assessment and anticoagulation checked; EP studies; TAVI).</li><li><strong>Interventional radiology</strong> (embolisation, TIPSS, thrombectomy).</li><li><strong>Emergency department:</strong> RSI for sick patients with limited information, then transfer.</li><li><strong>Dental sedation and GA</strong> (often for children and adults with learning disabilities; consent and capacity; shared airway).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> MRI hazards; ECT physiology and drug effects on seizure duration.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> preparing for a sedation list in MRI for children, including a safety briefing.</li></ul></div>","renal-liver-pom":"<h2 id=\"chronic-kidney-disease\">Chronic kidney disease</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Associated problems:</strong> hypertension, cardiovascular disease, anaemia, platelet dysfunction (uraemia; desmopressin helps), hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload, delayed gastric emptying, renal osteodystrophy, and difficult vascular access (<strong>protect arteriovenous fistulas</strong>: no cannulas, cuffs or tight wraps on that arm).</li><li><strong>Dialysis patients:</strong> timing of dialysis (usually the day before surgery; check potassium and fluid status afterwards), avoid nephrotoxins, and consider residual function.</li><li><strong>Drugs:</strong> avoid or reduce renally excreted drugs and active metabolites (<strong>morphine</strong> — M6G; <strong>pethidine</strong>; high doses of gabapentinoids; enoxaparin adjustments). <strong>Atracurium or cisatracurium</strong>, fentanyl, alfentanil, remifentanil, propofol are suitable. Suxamethonium is acceptable if potassium is normal (rise of about 0.5 mmol/L). Sugammadex is not recommended in severe renal impairment.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"liver-disease\">Liver disease</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Severity:</strong> <strong>Child–Pugh</strong> (bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites, encephalopathy: class A–C) and <strong>MELD</strong> (bilirubin, INR, creatinine ± sodium) predict perioperative mortality, which is high in decompensated cirrhosis.</li><li><strong>Problems:</strong> coagulopathy (rebalanced — use viscoelastic tests), thrombocytopenia, hypoalbuminaemia, ascites (reduced FRC; drain), encephalopathy (avoid sedatives), hyperdynamic circulation with low SVR, <strong>hepatorenal syndrome</strong>, <strong>hepatopulmonary syndrome</strong> (hypoxaemia from intrapulmonary shunts, worse upright), <strong>portopulmonary hypertension</strong>, varices (avoid oesophageal instrumentation), hypoglycaemia, alcohol withdrawal, malnutrition.</li><li><strong>Drugs:</strong> increased volume of distribution of water-soluble drugs, reduced clearance of hepatically metabolised drugs (benzodiazepines, opioids), reduced plasma cholinesterase; atracurium/cisatracurium and remifentanil are useful.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"transplantation\">Transplantation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Organ donation ethics:</strong> deceased donation after brainstem death (DBD) or circulatory death (DCD); living donation (donor safety and consent); respecting the donor's and family's wishes (opt-out legislation in parts of the UK, with family involvement).</li><li><strong>Anaesthesia for kidney transplant:</strong> optimise volume to ensure graft perfusion (MAP targets), avoid nephrotoxins; immunosuppression.</li><li><strong>Liver transplant:</strong> a specialist field (massive transfusion, reperfusion syndrome, coagulopathy).</li><li><strong>Patients with a transplanted organ having other surgery:</strong> continue immunosuppression (drug interactions and levels), strict asepsis (infection risk), renal toxicity of calcineurin inhibitors, and the <strong>denervated heart</strong> (a heart transplant responds to circulating catecholamines but <strong>not to atropine</strong>; use direct-acting drugs such as isoprenaline or adrenaline; slower response to hypovolaemia).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> drug choices in renal and hepatic failure; Child–Pugh; the denervated heart.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> preassessment of a patient on haemodialysis for a hernia repair.</li></ul></div>","research-appraisal":"<h2 id=\"framing-a-question\">Framing a question</h2>\n<p><strong>PICO</strong>: Population, Intervention, Comparator, Outcome (± time). Choose outcomes that matter to patients (mortality, days alive and out of hospital, disability-free survival, quality of recovery) and define them precisely; recognise uncertainty in measuring them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"critical-appraisal-key-questions\">Critical appraisal (key questions)</h2>\n<ol><li>Is the question clear and important?</li><li>Is the design appropriate (RCT, cohort, case–control, systematic review)?</li><li><strong>Randomisation and allocation concealment</strong> adequate? <strong>Blinding</strong>? Groups similar at baseline?</li><li><strong>Bias:</strong> selection, performance, detection, attrition, reporting; <strong>confounding</strong> in observational studies.</li><li><strong>Sample size and power</strong> (was the study powered for the primary outcome? a clinically important difference?)</li><li>Analysis: intention to treat; handling of missing data; appropriate tests; multiple comparisons.</li><li><strong>Results:</strong> effect size with <strong>confidence intervals</strong>, absolute and relative effects, NNT.</li><li><strong>Applicability</strong> to your patients, and <strong>clinical significance</strong> (a statistically significant difference may be too small to matter; a non-significant result may reflect low power).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"trials-and-their-limits\">Trials and their limits</h2>\n<p>Controls (placebo, active, usual care), randomisation and blinding, exclusion criteria (limit generalisability), surrogate outcomes, early stopping, industry funding and conflicts of interest, the gap between efficacy (ideal conditions) and effectiveness (real world). <strong>Pragmatic trials</strong>, registries and large national audits (NAPs, NELA) complement RCTs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"systematic-reviews-and-meta-analysis\">Systematic reviews and meta-analysis</h2>\n<p>Comprehensive searches, risk of bias assessment, heterogeneity (I<sup>2</sup>), fixed versus random effects, publication bias (funnel plots), and the certainty of evidence (GRADE). Pitfalls: pooling low-quality or heterogeneous studies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"governance-and-ethics\">Governance and ethics</h2>\n<p>Research ethics committee approval, <strong>informed consent</strong> (including emergency research processes when patients lack capacity), data protection, sponsorship, Good Clinical Practice, the roles of Trust R&amp;D and university departments, and translation of findings into practice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"communicating-evidence\">Communicating evidence</h2>\n<p>Explain risks using absolute numbers and natural frequencies (e.g. \"about 1 in 100 people\"), visual aids, and honest statements of uncertainty; national reports (e.g. MBRRACE for maternal outcomes) help set context.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> appraisal concepts; clinical versus statistical significance; design choice for a question.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient asks whether a new block is \"proven\": explain the evidence honestly.</li></ul></div>","respiratory-neuro-pom":"<h2 id=\"copd-and-asthma\">COPD and asthma</h2>\n<ul><li>Assess severity (exercise tolerance, exacerbations, home oxygen, admissions, spirometry, CO<sub>2</sub> retention on gases). Optimise: inhalers, treat infection, <strong>smoking cessation</strong>, pulmonary rehabilitation. Consider regional techniques. <strong>Asthma:</strong> well controlled before elective surgery; avoid triggers (NSAIDs in sensitive patients, histamine-releasing drugs, instrumentation in light planes); steroid cover if on long-term steroids.</li><li><strong>Postoperative pulmonary complications</strong> risk factors: age, COPD, smoking, upper abdominal and thoracic surgery, long duration, emergency surgery, obesity, low albumin. Reduce with protective ventilation, analgesia (regional), early mobilisation, physiotherapy, and avoiding residual neuromuscular block.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"obstructive-sleep-apnoea-osa-and-obesity-hypoventilation\">Obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) and obesity hypoventilation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Screen</strong> with <strong>STOP-BANG</strong> (snoring, tiredness, observed apnoeas, blood pressure, BMI &gt; 35, age &gt; 50, neck &gt; 40 cm, male); confirm with <strong>sleep studies</strong> (apnoea–hypopnoea index: mild 5–15, moderate 15–30, severe &gt; 30).</li><li><strong>Risks:</strong> difficult mask ventilation and intubation, postoperative apnoea and hypoxaemia (sensitivity to opioids and sedatives), pulmonary hypertension, arrhythmias.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> bring <strong>CPAP</strong> to hospital and use it after surgery; minimise opioids (regional, multimodal); avoid sedative premedication; extubate fully awake, sitting up; monitored bed for high-risk patients.</li><li><strong>Obesity hypoventilation syndrome</strong> (BMI &gt; 30 with daytime hypercapnia): higher risk; may need NIV.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"neuromuscular-diseases\">Neuromuscular diseases</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Key issues</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Myasthenia gravis</strong></td><td>Continue pyridostigmine (adjust doses), <strong>sensitive to non-depolarising NMBs</strong> (reduced doses, monitoring; sugammadex useful), <strong>resistant to suxamethonium</strong>; risk of postoperative respiratory failure (predictors: disease duration, dose of pyridostigmine, vital capacity, respiratory disease); myasthenic versus cholinergic crisis</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lambert–Eaton</strong></td><td>Sensitive to all NMBs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Muscular dystrophies (Duchenne, Becker)</strong></td><td><strong>Avoid suxamethonium and volatile agents</strong> (hyperkalaemic rhabdomyolysis); cardiomyopathy; restrictive lung disease</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Myotonic dystrophy</strong></td><td>Suxamethonium and anticholinesterases may trigger myotonia; sensitivity to sedatives and opioids; cardiac conduction defects; aspiration risk; avoid hypothermia and shivering</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guillain–Barré</strong></td><td>Autonomic instability, respiratory failure (monitor vital capacity), avoid suxamethonium (hyperkalaemia)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Motor neurone disease, multiple sclerosis</strong></td><td>Respiratory weakness, bulbar dysfunction; MS relapse with fever and stress; suxamethonium avoided in denervation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long-term spinal cord damage</strong></td><td>Autonomic dysreflexia, suxamethonium hyperkalaemia (see spinal injury)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"public-health\">Public health</h2>\n<p>Surgery is a \"<strong>teachable moment</strong>\": brief interventions for smoking, alcohol and obesity; referral to services; the effects of these on health and outcomes (wound healing, infection, pulmonary and cardiac complications).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> STOP-BANG; AHI grades; NMB sensitivities in each condition.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> planning anaesthesia for a patient with myasthenia gravis having a thymectomy, including postoperative care.</li></ul></div>","sedation-advanced":"<h2 id=\"levels-and-risks\">Levels and risks</h2>\n<p>Minimal, moderate (\"conscious\"), deep sedation and general anaesthesia form a continuum. <strong>Deep sedation</strong> carries airway and respiratory risks similar to GA and requires the same standards (an anaesthetist or appropriately trained practitioner, capnography, airway equipment).</p>\n<h2 id=\"drugs-and-techniques\">Drugs and techniques</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug / technique</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Propofol</strong> (bolus or <strong>TCI</strong> at low effect-site targets)</td><td>Rapid titration; deepens quickly; no analgesia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Remifentanil</strong> (TCI)</td><td>Analgesia and sedation; apnoea and rigidity at higher doses; synergy with propofol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Midazolam</strong></td><td>Anxiolysis and amnesia; slow offset in the elderly; flumazenil available</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dexmedetomidine</strong></td><td>Rousable sedation with little respiratory depression; bradycardia, hypotension; useful in awake craniotomy and ATI</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ketamine</strong></td><td>Dissociative sedation and analgesia; airway reflexes preserved; emergence phenomena</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nitrous oxide / Entonox</strong></td><td>Short procedures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alternative routes</strong></td><td>Intranasal (dexmedetomidine, midazolam, fentanyl, ketamine) and rectal (children), oral premedication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"standards-and-scoring\">Standards and scoring</h2>\n<ul><li>National guidance (e.g. the Academy of Medical Royal Colleges \"Safe sedation practice\" and RCoA/specialty guidance) sets standards for staff training, monitoring, equipment and recovery.</li><li>Scoring: RASS, Ramsay, OAA/S (adults); University of Michigan Sedation Scale (children).</li><li><strong>Discharge criteria:</strong> return to baseline consciousness, stable observations, pain and nausea controlled, escort for 24 hours, written advice.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"specific-settings\">Specific settings</h2>\n<p>Ophthalmic (brief, lying flat; avoid oversedation — patients may move unexpectedly), neurosurgical (awake craniotomy), dental (children and people with learning disabilities; conscious sedation standards), endoscopy (ERCP prone), cardiology (TAVI, ablations), radiology (MRI in children).</p>\n<h2 id=\"extremes-of-age-and-when-not-to-sedate\">Extremes of age and when not to sedate</h2>\n<p>Older patients: slower circulation, sensitivity, delirium. Children: unpredictable responses (paradoxical agitation, rapid airway obstruction). <strong>Sedation is inappropriate</strong> when the airway is at risk (e.g. severe OSA, full stomach, difficult airway), the patient cannot cooperate or lie still for the procedure, the procedure is too stimulating or long, or when staffing or location is unsuitable — choose GA or a regional technique instead.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> drug properties; standards; discharge criteria.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> asked to provide deep sedation for a prone ERCP in a patient with severe OSA: discuss alternatives.</li></ul></div>","sepsis-organ-support":"<h2 id=\"sepsis\">Sepsis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pathophysiology:</strong> a dysregulated host response: cytokine release, endothelial activation and glycocalyx damage (capillary leak), vasodilation (NO), microcirculatory dysfunction, mitochondrial dysfunction, coagulation activation (DIC), immunosuppression later.</li><li><strong>Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021 (key points):</strong> screen and treat urgently; <strong>antibiotics within 1 hour</strong> for probable septic shock; <strong>30 mL/kg crystalloid</strong> (balanced) in the first 3 hours for hypoperfusion (individualised with reassessment); <strong>noradrenaline</strong> first-line vasopressor to MAP ≥ 65 mmHg, adding <strong>vasopressin</strong>; hydrocortisone for ongoing vasopressor needs; lactate-guided resuscitation and capillary refill time; source control; restrictive transfusion (Hb 70 g/L); lung-protective ventilation; VTE and stress ulcer prophylaxis.</li><li><strong>Antimicrobials:</strong> principles of action and choice (see Primary), <strong>stewardship</strong> (cultures first, review at 48–72 hours, de-escalate, stop), antivirals and antifungals in selected patients (immunocompromised, specific infections).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"shock\">Shock</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Profile</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hypovolaemic</td><td>Haemorrhage, GI losses</td><td>↓ CO, ↑ SVR, ↓ filling</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Distributive</strong></td><td>Sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic, adrenal crisis</td><td>↑/normal CO (early sepsis), <strong>↓ SVR</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cardiogenic</td><td>MI, cardiomyopathy, arrhythmia, valve failure</td><td>↓ CO, ↑ SVR, ↑ filling pressures</td></tr><tr><td>Obstructive</td><td>PE, tamponade, tension pneumothorax</td><td>↓ CO, specific signs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Assessment: clinical examination (perfusion, capillary refill, mentation, urine output), lactate, echocardiography, cardiac output monitoring, ScvO<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cardiovascular-drugs-and-devices\">Cardiovascular drugs and devices</h2>\n<p>Vasopressors (noradrenaline, vasopressin, phenylephrine), inotropes (dobutamine, adrenaline, milrinone, levosimendan), and mechanical support (IABP, Impella, VA-ECMO) in cardiogenic shock; pulmonary hypertension and RV failure management; blood pressure targets individualised (e.g. higher in chronic hypertension).</p>\n<h2 id=\"investigations\">Investigations</h2>\n<p>Interpret blood tests (lactate, procalcitonin cautiously, troponin, coagulation), blood gases, imaging (CXR, CT, ultrasound), echocardiography, and microbiology.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-deteriorating-patient\">The deteriorating patient</h2>\n<p><strong>NEWS2</strong>-triggered escalation, critical care outreach, rapid response teams, early senior review and treatment escalation plans. Scoring systems (SOFA, APACHE II) quantify severity in ICU (for audit and research more than individual prognosis).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> SSC 2021 recommendations; shock profiles; vasopressor choices.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> receiving a referral about a hypotensive septic patient on the ward and deciding on admission and ceilings of care.</li></ul></div>","specialty-ortho-plastics-urology":"<h2 id=\"major-orthopaedic-surgery\">Major orthopaedic surgery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Primary and revision arthroplasty</strong> of hip and knee: spinal or GA with regional analgesia (periarticular infiltration, adductor canal and other blocks), <strong>tranexamic acid</strong>, <strong>cell salvage</strong> for revision surgery with major expected blood loss, VTE prophylaxis, early mobilisation; bone cement implantation syndrome risk with cemented components.</li><li><strong>Pelvic and acetabular surgery:</strong> major blood loss, prolonged prone or lateral positioning, nerve injury.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"plastic-and-reconstructive-surgery-free-flaps\">Plastic and reconstructive surgery: free flaps</h2>\n<ul><li>Free tissue transfer depends on <strong>flow through the microvascular anastomosis</strong>. Blood flow ∝ perfusion pressure / resistance, and viscosity (Hagen–Poiseuille).</li><li><strong>Goals:</strong> a <strong>high cardiac output</strong>, normal or slightly high MAP, <strong>vasodilated periphery</strong> (warmth, analgesia, avoid vasoconstriction from hypothermia, hypovolaemia, pain and hypocapnia), <strong>haematocrit about 0.30–0.35</strong> (lower viscosity while preserving oxygen content), normothermia, adequate but not excessive fluids.</li><li><strong>Vasopressors:</strong> often feared but modest doses to maintain perfusion pressure are acceptable in many centres; avoid hypotension.</li><li>Long procedures: pressure areas, heat loss, positioning, and postoperative flap monitoring.</li><li><strong>Burns reconstruction</strong> and dressing changes: see burns.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"urology\">Urology</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>TURP syndrome:</strong> absorption of large volumes of hypotonic irrigation fluid (<strong>1.5% glycine</strong>) through opened prostatic venous sinuses → <strong>dilutional hyponatraemia</strong>, fluid overload (hypertension, bradycardia, pulmonary oedema), cerebral oedema (confusion, seizures, coma), and glycine toxicity (transient <strong>visual disturbance</strong>, ammonia). Risk increases with resection time (&gt; 60 min), high irrigation pressure and large glands. <strong>Spinal anaesthesia</strong> allows early detection of confusion. Treatment: stop the procedure, fluid restriction, diuretics for overload, <strong>hypertonic saline</strong> for severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (correct cautiously). Bipolar resection with saline irrigation has largely replaced glycine and removes the hyponatraemia risk (fluid overload remains).</li><li>Other issues: lithotomy position, bladder perforation (shoulder tip pain), obturator nerve stimulation (adductor jerk), bacteraemia, bleeding.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"day-surgery-organisation-and-advances\">Day surgery: organisation and advances</h2>\n<ul><li>Most elective surgery can be done as day cases, including many major procedures with enhanced recovery (e.g. laparoscopic cholecystectomy, hernia, some arthroplasty and spinal surgery). <strong>BADS</strong> and GIRFT set targets and directories of procedures.</li><li><strong>Organisation:</strong> dedicated units, nurse-led preassessment with protocols, efficient scheduling, audit of <strong>unplanned admission</strong>, cancellation and patient-reported outcomes, and follow-up telephone calls.</li><li><strong>Advances:</strong> short-acting spinal agents (chloroprocaine, prilocaine) for day-case lower limb and perineal surgery, opioid-sparing multimodal analgesia, and remote monitoring.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> free flap haemodynamic goals; TURP syndrome features; tranexamic acid and cell salvage use.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient becomes confused and bradycardic 50 minutes into a TURP under spinal: diagnose and manage.</li></ul></div>","spinal-injury":"<h2 id=\"acute-injury\">Acute injury</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> NICE NG41 criteria for imaging; <strong>CT</strong> is first-line in adults with suspected cervical injury; MRI for cord and ligament injury.</li><li><strong>Airway:</strong> manual in-line stabilisation (remove the front of the collar), video laryngoscopy, minimal neck movement; awake fibreoptic intubation in selected cooperative patients.</li><li><strong>High cervical injuries</strong> (above C3–5) impair diaphragmatic function → early ventilation; <strong>lower cervical</strong> injuries cause intercostal paralysis (vital capacity falls; monitor it and intubate early if falling).</li><li><strong>Neurogenic shock</strong> (injuries above about T6): loss of sympathetic tone → <strong>hypotension with bradycardia</strong> (warm peripheries); treat with fluids cautiously, vasopressors (noradrenaline), atropine. Maintain MAP (often 85–90 mmHg for several days in acute cord injury) to protect cord perfusion.</li><li><strong>Spinal shock:</strong> temporary loss of all reflexes below the injury (hours to weeks) — flaccid paralysis.</li><li><strong>Suxamethonium:</strong> safe in the first 24–48 hours; <strong>avoid after about 72 hours</strong> for many months (hyperkalaemia from receptor upregulation).</li><li>Also: paralytic ileus, urinary retention, pressure areas, thromboembolism, hypothermia (poikilothermia), and the need for early specialist spinal unit involvement.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"chronic-spinal-cord-injury-from-weeks-after-injury\">Chronic spinal cord injury (from weeks after injury)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Autonomic dysreflexia</strong> (lesions above about <strong>T6</strong>): a stimulus below the level (bladder distension, bowel, surgery, labour) triggers massive unopposed sympathetic discharge → <strong>severe hypertension</strong>, reflex bradycardia, sweating and flushing above the lesion, headache; risk of stroke and seizures. Prevent with adequate anaesthesia (GA, spinal) even for \"painless\" procedures below the level; treat by removing the stimulus, sitting up, and rapid-acting vasodilators.</li><li>Respiratory impairment (weak cough, restrictive defect), spasticity and contractures (positioning), chronic pain, osteoporosis, recurrent UTIs, pressure ulcers, and hyperkalaemic response to suxamethonium.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> neurogenic versus spinal shock; timing of suxamethonium risk; autonomic dysreflexia mechanism.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient with T4 paraplegia listed for cystoscopy \"without anaesthesia because they have no sensation\": explain why you disagree.</li></ul></div>","sustainability-medication":"<h2 id=\"sustainability\">Sustainability</h2>\n<ul><li>The NHS aims for net-zero carbon emissions; anaesthesia contributes through <strong>inhaled agents</strong> (desflurane and nitrous oxide especially), energy use, single-use equipment and medicines waste.</li><li><strong>Actions:</strong> avoid desflurane (removed from routine use in many UK hospitals); decommission nitrous oxide manifolds and fix leaks; <strong>low fresh gas flows</strong>; TIVA and regional anaesthesia where appropriate; reusable equipment when safe; reduce drug waste (not drawing up unused emergency drugs routinely); switch off equipment; consider travel and procurement. Balance environmental benefit against patient safety.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"medicines-management\">Medicines management</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Existing medications:</strong> which to continue (β-blockers, statins, antiepileptics, Parkinson's drugs, steroids, most inhalers), which to stop or adjust (anticoagulants, antiplatelets per risk, ACEi/ARB on the day, SGLT2 inhibitors, sulphonylureas, MAOIs by specialist decision, lithium), and when to restart.</li><li><strong>Antibiotics and infection prevention:</strong> surgical prophylaxis timing and choice, stewardship, and measures to prevent surgical site infection (hair removal by clipping, skin preparation, normothermia, glucose control, oxygenation).</li><li><strong>Proactive safety:</strong> risk assessment of likelihood and severity (e.g. FMEA), standardised drug concentrations, labelling and connectors (NRFit), and wrong-site block prevention.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"drug-toxicity-additives-and-mh\">Drug toxicity, additives and MH</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Toxicity:</strong> causes (overdose, accumulation in organ failure, interactions, pharmacogenetics) and avoidance (dose checking, therapeutic monitoring).</li><li><strong>Drug additives and excipients:</strong> preservatives (e.g. metabisulfite, benzyl alcohol — avoid neuraxially), latex, allergens (egg and soya in propofol are not now considered contraindications for most patients), and lipid load from propofol.</li><li><strong>Malignant hyperthermia:</strong> see the Primary note; Stage 2 emphasis on leadership of the crisis and referral for testing.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> GWP of agents; medicines to continue or stop; excipients.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a colleague challenges your use of desflurane: discuss the evidence and alternatives.</li></ul></div>","tbi-neurocritical":"<h2 id=\"traumatic-brain-injury\">Traumatic brain injury</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Primary injury</strong> occurs at impact; management targets <strong>secondary injury</strong> from hypoxia, hypotension, hyper- and hypocapnia, hyperglycaemia, hyperthermia, seizures and raised ICP.</li><li><strong>Targets</strong> (typical UK practice; follow local neurocritical care protocols): SpO<sub>2</sub> &gt; 94% / PaO<sub>2</sub> &gt; 13 kPa; PaCO<sub>2</sub> 4.5–5.0 kPa; MAP ≥ 80–90 mmHg to achieve <strong>CPP 60–70 mmHg</strong>; ICP &lt; 20–22 mmHg; glucose 6–10 mmol/L; temperature &lt; 37.5°C; sodium high-normal; head-up 30°; adequate sedation and analgesia; seizure prophylaxis in selected patients.</li><li><strong>Tiered ICP management:</strong> sedation, CSF drainage, osmotherapy, mild hyperventilation, neuromuscular blockade, barbiturates, decompressive craniectomy.</li><li><strong>Surgery:</strong> evacuation of extradural (lucid interval, arterial bleed) and subdural haematomas; urgent transfer to the neurosurgical centre (\"time is brain\").</li><li><strong>Coagulopathy</strong> and <strong>hypotension</strong> are common in multiple trauma; resuscitation with blood and permissive hypotension are <strong>not</strong> appropriate when there is a severe head injury.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"aneurysmal-subarachnoid-haemorrhage\">Aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage</h2>\n<ul><li>\"Thunderclap\" headache; <strong>World Federation of Neurosurgical Societies (WFNS) grade</strong> (based on GCS and motor deficit).</li><li><strong>Complications:</strong> rebleeding (early securing of the aneurysm by coiling or clipping), hydrocephalus (EVD), <strong>delayed cerebral ischaemia / vasospasm</strong> (days 4–14; <strong>nimodipine</strong> orally; maintain euvolaemia and adequate blood pressure; endovascular treatment), hyponatraemia (cerebral salt wasting or SIADH), neurogenic pulmonary oedema and stunned myocardium.</li><li><strong>Anaesthesia for coiling/clipping:</strong> avoid hypertension before securing (rupture risk), controlled emergence, heparin during coiling, readiness for intraoperative rupture.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"status-epilepticus\">Status epilepticus</h2>\n<p>Continuous seizure &gt; 5 minutes or recurrent seizures without recovery: ABC, glucose, <strong>benzodiazepine</strong> (IV lorazepam or buccal/IM midazolam), then <strong>second-line</strong> (levetiracetam, phenytoin or valproate), then <strong>anaesthesia</strong> (propofol, thiopental or midazolam infusion) with intubation and EEG monitoring; find the cause.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transfer-of-the-brain-injured-patient\">Transfer of the brain-injured patient</h2>\n<p>Association of Anaesthetists 2019 guideline: stabilise, intubate if GCS ≤ 8 or deteriorating, secure lines, targets as above, avoid delay, experienced escort, full monitoring including capnography and invasive BP.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> physiological targets; vasospasm timing and nimodipine; status epilepticus steps.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> preparing a patient with an acute subdural haematoma for transfer, and handing over to the neurosurgical team.</li></ul></div>","thoracic-olv":"<h2 id=\"lung-isolation\">Lung isolation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Indications:</strong> absolute — isolation to prevent contamination (infection, massive haemorrhage), control of ventilation (bronchopleural fistula, unilateral bullae), unilateral lung lavage; relative — surgical exposure (lung resection, oesophagectomy, thoracic spine, VATS).</li><li><strong>Double-lumen tubes (DLTs):</strong> left-sided DLTs are used for most procedures (the right upper lobe bronchus arises close to the carina, making right-sided DLTs harder to position). Size by height and imaging (women about 35–37 Fr, men 39–41 Fr). <strong>Confirm position with a flexible bronchoscope</strong> after insertion and after positioning.</li><li><strong>Bronchial blockers:</strong> useful with difficult airways, existing single-lumen tubes, nasal intubation or when postoperative ventilation is needed; slower lung deflation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"physiology-of-one-lung-ventilation-olv\">Physiology of one-lung ventilation (OLV)</h2>\n<ul><li>In the <strong>lateral position</strong> with the non-dependent lung collapsed, blood flowing through it becomes <strong>shunt</strong>. <strong>Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong> diverts flow to the ventilated lung, and gravity helps.</li><li>HPV is inhibited by volatile agents (weakly, dose-dependently), vasodilators and alkalosis; TIVA may preserve it slightly better.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"managing-hypoxaemia-during-olv\">Managing hypoxaemia during OLV</h2>\n<ol><li>Increase FiO<sub>2</sub> to 1.0; check the tube position with the bronchoscope; suction.</li><li>Optimise ventilation of the dependent lung: <strong>recruitment</strong>, appropriate <strong>PEEP</strong>, avoid excessive airway pressures (which divert blood to the non-ventilated lung).</li><li><strong>CPAP (or oxygen insufflation) to the non-ventilated lung</strong> (warn the surgeon).</li><li>Intermittent two-lung ventilation; surgical clamping of the pulmonary artery in pneumonectomy.</li><li><strong>Protective ventilation:</strong> tidal volumes about 4–6 mL/kg predicted body weight to the single lung, PEEP, plateau pressure &lt; 25 cmH<sub>2</sub>O, to reduce acute lung injury (especially after pneumonectomy).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"procedures-and-postoperative-care\">Procedures and postoperative care</h2>\n<ul><li>Lobectomy (often <strong>VATS</strong> or robotic), pneumonectomy (fluid restriction: post-pneumonectomy pulmonary oedema; avoid excessive fluid), rigid bronchoscopy (shared airway, jet ventilation), mediastinoscopy (compression of the innominate artery — right radial arterial line or pulse oximeter), oesophagectomy, pleurodesis and decortication.</li><li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> paravertebral catheter or erector spinae block (as effective as thoracic epidural for many procedures with fewer side effects), thoracic epidural in selected cases, multimodal systemic analgesia.</li><li><strong>Complications:</strong> air leak, atelectasis, pneumonia, arrhythmias (AF), bleeding, bronchopleural fistula, ARDS, and nerve injury (phrenic, recurrent laryngeal).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> DLT choice and confirmation; HPV; the stepwise management of hypoxaemia during OLV.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> desaturation to 85% during one-lung ventilation for a lobectomy: talk through your response with the surgeon.</li></ul></div>","tiva-positioning":"<h2 id=\"tiva-models\">TIVA models</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Propofol:</strong> <strong>Marsh</strong> (weight-based; plasma targeting; overdoses the elderly and obese if actual weight is used), <strong>Schnider</strong> (age, height, weight, sex, lean body mass; effect-site targeting; problems with LBM formulae in morbid obesity), <strong>Eleveld</strong> (a newer general-purpose model from a large pooled dataset covering a wide range of ages and weights, including children and obese patients).</li><li><strong>Remifentanil: Minto</strong> (age, LBM); <strong>Eleveld</strong> remifentanil model.</li><li>In <strong>obesity</strong>, follow model-specific guidance (e.g. adjusted weight for Marsh, or Eleveld) and use processed EEG.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"safe-tiva-association-of-anaesthetists-siva-2018\">Safe TIVA (Association of Anaesthetists/SIVA 2018)</h2>\n<ul><li>Dedicated IV cannula visible throughout, <strong>anti-reflux valves and non-return valves</strong>, minimal dead space; syringes clearly labelled; programmed pumps with correct drug concentrations; alarms on.</li><li><strong>Processed EEG</strong> monitoring when neuromuscular blockers are used (monitoring standards 2021).</li><li>Beware disconnection, occlusion, extravasation, empty syringes and drug errors — common causes of <strong>awareness</strong> in NAP5 (which found TIVA overrepresented in awareness reports, often from errors and transfer between areas).</li><li><strong>Other NAP5 risk factors:</strong> neuromuscular block, obstetric GA, cardiac surgery, RSI, obesity, difficult airway, junior anaesthetists, out-of-hours. Awareness can cause psychological harm (PTSD); follow up with an apology, explanation and support.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"positioning\">Positioning</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Physiological effects</th><th>Injuries</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Prone</strong></td><td>↓ venous return if the abdomen is compressed (use supports), ↑ FRC and oxygenation, access to the airway limited</td><td><strong>Eye injury and postoperative visual loss</strong> (ischaemic optic neuropathy, central retinal artery occlusion from pressure), facial pressure injury, brachial plexus, breasts and genitals, air embolism (spinal)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lateral</strong></td><td>V/Q mismatch (dependent lung better perfused, non-dependent better ventilated under IPPV)</td><td>Axillary neurovascular compression (axillary roll), ear, eye</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sitting / beach-chair</strong></td><td>Hypotension and cerebral hypoperfusion (measure BP at head level), VAE</td><td>Cerebral ischaemia, pressure injuries</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lithotomy</strong></td><td>↑ venous return on raising legs, ↓ on lowering; ↓ FRC</td><td><strong>Common peroneal nerve</strong>, lower limb compartment syndrome (long procedures)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trendelenburg</strong></td><td>↑ ICP and IOP, ↓ FRC, airway oedema</td><td>Brachial plexus (shoulder braces), sliding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Obese patients are at greater risk of pressure injury, rhabdomyolysis, and respiratory compromise in all positions.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> model inputs and pitfalls; NAP5 findings; positioning injuries.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> preparing a patient for prone spinal surgery, including a checklist for positioning.</li></ul></div>","tracheostomy-laryngectomy":"<h2 id=\"indications\">Indications</h2>\n<p>Upper airway obstruction (tumour, trauma, infection), prolonged ventilation or weaning, airway protection and secretion management (neurological disease), and as part of major head and neck surgery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"techniques\">Techniques</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Surgical</strong> (open) or <strong>percutaneous dilatational</strong> (in ICU, often with bronchoscopic guidance and ultrasound).</li><li>Anaesthetic issues: shared airway, withdrawing the tracheal tube just above the stoma under direct vision, fire risk with diathermy into the trachea (reduce FiO<sub>2</sub>), confirming placement with capnography, securing the tube.</li><li><strong>Early complications:</strong> bleeding, misplacement (pre-tracheal), pneumothorax, surgical emphysema, tube displacement (the tract is immature for about 7 days). <strong>Late:</strong> tracheal stenosis, tracheomalacia, tracheo-innominate fistula (sentinel bleed), tracheo-oesophageal fistula.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"emergencies-national-tracheostomy-safety-project-principles\">Emergencies (National Tracheostomy Safety Project principles)</h2>\n<p><strong>Know whether the patient has a patent upper airway</strong> (tracheostomy) or <strong>not</strong> (<strong>laryngectomy</strong> — the trachea is sutured to the skin as an end stoma; the patient cannot be oxygenated or intubated from above). Bedhead signs show which applies.</p>\n<p>For a patient with breathing difficulty and a tracheostomy:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Call for help</strong> (airway-trained staff); apply <strong>high-flow oxygen to both the face and the tracheostomy</strong> (only the stoma for a laryngectomy).</li><li>Remove any speaking valve or cap; remove the <strong>inner tube</strong> (a blocked inner cannula is the commonest problem).</li><li><strong>Pass a suction catheter:</strong> if it passes easily, the tube is patent — consider partial obstruction and continue assessment.</li><li>If it will not pass: <strong>deflate the cuff</strong>; if still not improving, <strong>remove the tracheostomy tube</strong>.</li><li>Oxygenate via the <strong>upper airway</strong> (face mask/SGA, with the stoma covered) and/or via the <strong>stoma</strong> (paediatric face mask or LMA over the stoma).</li><li>Secondary emergency oxygenation: oral intubation (with a longer uncut tube advanced beyond the stoma) or intubation of the stoma with a small tube, guided by a bougie or fibrescope.</li></ol>\n<p>Children have separate algorithms (a small tube, stay sutures in new tracheostomies).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> early and late complications; differences between a tracheostomy and laryngectomy.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a simulated blocked tracheostomy on the ward: follow the algorithm aloud and lead the team.</li></ul></div>","transfer-advanced":"<h2 id=\"before-you-go\">Before you go</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Is the transfer necessary and is the patient ready?</strong> Stabilise (secure the airway, lines, drains, splints), but for <strong>time-critical</strong> transfers (e.g. extradural haematoma, aortic dissection) avoid delaying definitive treatment for non-essential interventions.</li><li><strong>Pre-emptive interventions:</strong> intubate if the airway may deteriorate; chest drains for pneumothorax before air transfer; arterial line; adequate sedation and analgesia; secure everything.</li><li><strong>Packaging:</strong> vacuum mattress, secured lines, pumps and ventilator mounted safely; warmed.</li><li><strong>Equipment:</strong> a transfer ventilator (know its oxygen consumption), full monitoring with <strong>waveform capnography and invasive BP</strong>, spare batteries, suction, drugs (sedation, vasopressors, resuscitation drugs), airway kit, and <strong>calculated oxygen supply with a margin</strong> (at least 50% extra, or double).</li><li><strong>Personnel:</strong> an experienced doctor and assistant, trained in transfer; regional critical care network protocols and retrieval services.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"during-transfer\">During transfer</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Physiological effects:</strong> acceleration/deceleration (venous pooling or increased return, raised ICP), vibration, noise (alarms may not be heard), temperature changes, motion sickness; in aircraft, <strong>altitude</strong> (gas expansion in closed spaces, lower PaO<sub>2</sub>).</li><li><strong>Problems in transit:</strong> accidental extubation or tube displacement, ventilator or oxygen failure, loss of IV access, cardiovascular instability, agitation. Stop the vehicle if an intervention is needed. Have a plan and the kit at hand.</li><li><strong>Deaths in transit:</strong> follow the ambulance service and legal procedures (the coroner or procurator fiscal).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"communication-law-and-ethics\">Communication, law and ethics</h2>\n<ul><li>Clear handover (SBAR) at both ends; document the transfer (observations, drugs, events).</li><li>Explain to the patient and relatives why transfer is needed; consent where possible; confidentiality.</li><li><strong>Audit and incident reporting</strong> for all transfers; regional standards and training.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> oxygen calculations; physiological effects of transport; monitoring standards.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> during a transfer your ventilated patient's SpO<sub>2</sub> falls to 85% on the motorway: talk through your actions.</li></ul></div>","trauma-haemorrhage":"<h2 id=\"trauma-systems-and-the-team\">Trauma systems and the team</h2>\n<p>Regional <strong>major trauma networks</strong> (major trauma centres and units), triage tools, pre-alerts. In the resuscitation room the anaesthetist usually leads the airway and often the resuscitation, within a team with a clear leader, defined roles, and closed-loop communication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"damage-control-resuscitation\">Damage control resuscitation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Haemorrhage control</strong> (tourniquets, pelvic binder, surgery, interventional radiology) is the priority.</li><li><strong>Permissive hypotension</strong> (e.g. systolic 80–90 mmHg, or a palpable radial pulse) in penetrating and blunt trauma <strong>without traumatic brain injury</strong>, until bleeding is controlled.</li><li><strong>Blood-based resuscitation</strong> with <strong>early plasma and platelets</strong> in balanced ratios (about 1:1:1 initially), minimal crystalloid, and <strong>tranexamic acid</strong> (1 g over 10 minutes then 1 g over 8 hours) <strong>within 3 hours</strong> of injury (CRASH-2). Then <strong>goal-directed</strong> replacement guided by laboratory and <strong>viscoelastic</strong> tests.</li><li><strong>Prevent and treat the lethal triad</strong> (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy) plus <strong>hypocalcaemia</strong> (citrate).</li><li><strong>Rapid infusion devices</strong> with warming; large-bore access (including IO and central); cell salvage in selected cases.</li><li><strong>Trauma-induced coagulopathy:</strong> endogenous (shock and tissue injury activate protein C, hyperfibrinolysis, fibrinogen consumption) plus dilution, hypothermia and acidosis.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"damage-control-surgery\">Damage control surgery</h2>\n<p>Abbreviated surgery to stop bleeding and contamination, then <strong>resuscitation in ICU</strong> (warming, correction of coagulopathy), then definitive surgery. Beware abdominal compartment syndrome.</p>\n<h2 id=\"specific-injuries\">Specific injuries</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Chest:</strong> tension pneumothorax (decompress), massive haemothorax (drain, surgery if &gt; 1,500 mL initially or ongoing loss), flail chest and pulmonary contusion (analgesia — regional blocks such as erector spinae or epidural — and ventilatory support), cardiac tamponade, aortic injury. <strong>Resuscitative thoracotomy</strong> for traumatic arrest in selected cases (penetrating chest trauma).</li><li><strong>Abdomen:</strong> CT in stable patients; FAST; laparotomy for instability; splenic and liver injuries (embolisation).</li><li><strong>Pelvis:</strong> binder, interventional radiology or pre-peritoneal packing; beware urethral injury (catheter).</li><li><strong>Limbs:</strong> fractures, compartment syndrome (pain out of proportion; regional anaesthesia and monitoring — discuss with surgeons), fat embolism.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"regional-anaesthesia-in-trauma\">Regional anaesthesia in trauma</h2>\n<p>Useful for rib fractures, hip and femoral fractures (fascia iliaca), and limb injuries; consider coagulopathy, compartment syndrome risk (communicate and document), and the need for neurological assessment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-metabolic-response-to-trauma\">The metabolic response to trauma</h2>\n<p>Catabolism, hyperglycaemia, fluid retention; the ebb (shock) and flow (hypermetabolic) phases.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> permissive hypotension exceptions; TXA timing; ratios; lethal triad.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> lead the resuscitation of a hypotensive motorcyclist with a pelvic fracture, communicating with the trauma team and blood bank.</li></ul></div>","valve-disease":"<h2 id=\"assessment\">Assessment</h2>\n<p>Symptoms (angina, syncope, breathlessness), functional capacity, examination (murmurs, signs of heart failure), <strong>echocardiography</strong> (valve area, gradients, ventricular function, pulmonary pressures), and the timing of any valve intervention relative to non-cardiac surgery (the ESC 2022 guideline recommends intervention before elective surgery for symptomatic severe AS where feasible).</p>\n<h2 id=\"haemodynamic-goals\">Haemodynamic goals</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesion</th><th>Heart rate</th><th>Rhythm</th><th>Preload</th><th>Afterload (SVR)</th><th>Contractility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aortic stenosis</strong></td><td><strong>Low–normal</strong> (60–80)</td><td><strong>Sinus</strong> (atrial kick vital)</td><td>Maintain</td><td><strong>Maintain</strong> (avoid falls)</td><td>Maintain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mitral stenosis</strong></td><td><strong>Low</strong> (maximise filling time)</td><td>Sinus if possible; rate-control AF</td><td>Maintain</td><td>Maintain; avoid ↑ PVR (hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis)</td><td>Maintain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aortic regurgitation</strong></td><td><strong>Normal–high</strong> (less diastolic time for regurgitation)</td><td></td><td>Maintain</td><td><strong>Reduce</strong> (forward flow)</td><td>Maintain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mitral regurgitation</strong></td><td>Normal–high</td><td></td><td>Maintain</td><td><strong>Reduce</strong></td><td>Maintain</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"aortic-stenosis\">Aortic stenosis</h2>\n<ul><li>Severe AS: <strong>valve area &lt; 1.0 cm<sup>2</sup></strong>, <strong>mean gradient &gt; 40 mmHg</strong>, peak velocity &gt; 4 m/s (beware low-flow, low-gradient AS with poor LV function).</li><li>The hypertrophied LV has a <strong>fixed stroke volume</strong>, high oxygen demand and depends on diastolic coronary perfusion. <strong>Hypotension → reduced coronary perfusion → ischaemia → further hypotension</strong> (a downward spiral). Treat hypotension promptly with a <strong>vasoconstrictor</strong> (phenylephrine or metaraminol); avoid tachycardia.</li><li>Neuraxial anaesthesia is not absolutely contraindicated but sudden sympathectomy is dangerous: titrated epidural or low-dose spinal with invasive monitoring, or a carefully titrated GA.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"tavi-and-other-structural-procedures\">TAVI and other structural procedures</h2>\n<p><strong>Transcatheter aortic valve implantation</strong> (usually transfemoral, often under local anaesthesia and sedation) for patients at higher surgical risk or older age. Complications: vascular injury, stroke, heart block (pacing), paravalvular leak, annular rupture, coronary occlusion, tamponade. <strong>Rapid ventricular pacing</strong> reduces cardiac output during deployment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"infective-endocarditis-prophylaxis\">Infective endocarditis prophylaxis</h2>\n<p>In the UK (NICE), antibiotic prophylaxis against endocarditis is <strong>not routinely recommended</strong> for dental or other procedures; for patients at high risk (prosthetic valves, previous endocarditis, some congenital heart disease) undergoing procedures at an infected site, antibiotics that cover the likely organisms are given. Follow current national and local guidance.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the goals table; severe AS criteria; why AS tolerates tachycardia and vasodilation poorly.</li><li><strong>FCPE:</strong> a patient with severe AS needs a hip fracture repair: explain your plan to the patient and the orthogeriatrician.</li></ul></div>"};
